"""Micro-skills written in Claude Code, in the micro.py format from HANDOVER.md.

Keyed (macro skill name, micro-skill name). Merge into micro.py when the
original build folder is back; apply_micro.py writes these straight into the
workbook in the meantime.
"""

MICRO = {

# ── 1. ASSESSMENT · Synthesise findings from multiple sources into one profile ──

("Synthesise findings from multiple sources into one profile","Identify where sources converge"): {
"why":   "Convergence is the strongest evidence an assessment produces: the same pattern seen by different people, in different settings, with different methods. Triangulation, the idea that agreement across methods beats any single method (Denzin, The Research Act, 1978), is the logic of every integrated report. Get it wrong and you claim agreement that is only surface. 'Teacher and parent both mention attention' is not convergence if one means daydreaming and the other means leaving the seat.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor lines sources up before writing. Many do it on one page: sources down the side, domains across the top. Notice whether they check that two sources describe the same behaviour, or only use the same word.\n\nWRITE DOWN — every source used (interview, rating scale, observation, standardised test, work sample, file) and which domains each one spoke to.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'how did you know the teacher's and parent's reports were about the same thing and not just the same label?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — building the source-by-domain grid from the file, and marking every cell where two or more sources point the same way, with the specific evidence quoted.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the judgement on whether each apparent agreement is real convergence or just shared vocabulary.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — the grid is mechanical and teaches you the file. Judging whether agreement is genuine is the clinical skill, and it is the one you will be tested on.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — present the grid in supervision and state each convergence aloud: which sources, which behaviour, which setting, how you know they mean the same thing.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — convergence you count rather than check. Three sources that all rely on the same parent report are one source, not three.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'the rating scale and the interview both came from mum on the same day — is that two sources?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — build the grid, write the convergent findings into the integration section with each source named in the sentence, and bring it to supervision.\n\nPASS TEST — Convergence is stated rather than assumed\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the integration section of the report and the grid as a working paper on the case file. Log against 1. Assessment.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — 'all sources indicate attention difficulties' with no named sources, no settings and no check that the behaviours match.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain to a peer the difference between convergence of label and convergence of behaviour, and why sources that share an origin (the same informant, the same day, the same form) do not independently confirm each other.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you find you overstated agreement after the report is drafted: rewrite the sentence to name the actual sources, and weaken the claim to what the evidence carries.",
"error": "Writing 'findings were consistent across sources' without naming the sources or checking they described the same behaviour.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Identify where sources diverge and say so', 'Judge which source to weight and why', and into Formulation, where convergent evidence carries the working hypothesis.",
"evidence":"Report — integration section · grid on case file · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment"},

("Synthesise findings from multiple sources into one profile","Identify where sources diverge and say so"): {
"why":   "Divergence between sources is information, not noise. A child who is described as anxious at home and settled at school is telling you something about context. The cross-informant meta-analysis by Achenbach, McConaughy and Howell (Psychological Bulletin, 1987) found only modest agreement between different types of informant; exact figures are in the paper — check before quoting. A report that smooths divergence away hides the most useful finding and misleads the reader about how certain you are.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor words a disagreement between sources in the report. Notice whether they name both sides, or quietly pick one.\n\nWRITE DOWN — each point of divergence in the case: which sources, which domain, which setting, and the likely explanations your supervisor considered.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'when the teacher and parent disagree, how do you decide whether it's the child, the setting, or the informant?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — listing every divergence on the grid and drafting the possible explanations for each: different setting, different demands, different informant threshold, different time period.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the wording that goes in the report, because divergence can land badly with a parent or school who feels disbelieved.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — generating explanations is thinking you can practise safely. Wording that will be read by the family carries relational risk.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — draft the divergence paragraph yourself and read it aloud in supervision before it goes in the report.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — divergence resolved by picking the professional source over the parent, or the adult over the child, without a reason. That is a bias, not a judgement.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'you've gone with the teacher's view there. What would the parent say if they read this sentence?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — identify each divergence, offer at least one explanation grounded in the setting or task demands, and write it into the report in neutral language.\n\nPASS TEST — Discrepancy is named rather than smoothed away\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the integration section of the report, marked in your Appendix 5 entry as the place divergence was handled. Log against 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice (PSI Code 4.2.6).\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a profile that reads as if everyone agreed, when your grid shows they did not.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain why informant disagreement is expected rather than a problem, and describe the context-based reading of it (De Los Reyes & Kazdin, Psychological Bulletin, 2005, on informant discrepancies).\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when a family objects that the report calls them wrong: show them the sentence, explain that both accounts are reported as true in their setting, and amend wording that implies blame.",
"error": "Reporting only the account that fits the hypothesis and leaving the contradicting one out.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Analyse informant agreement and discrepancy' (rating scales), into Formulation, and into Ethical Practice — honest reporting of uncertainty.",
"evidence":"Report — integration section · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Synthesise findings from multiple sources into one profile","Judge which source to weight and why"): {
"why":   "Sources are not equal, and pretending they are is its own error. A standardised score has known reliability; a single twenty-minute observation may have caught an unusual day; a parent knows the child across years but not in class. Weighting is unavoidable, so the professional task is to make it explicit. An unstated weighting cannot be challenged, and one that cannot be challenged cannot be defended at a review meeting or complaint.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — the moment your supervisor treats one finding as more telling than another. Listen for the reason: reliability, proximity to the concern, the setting it came from, or how recent it is.\n\nWRITE DOWN — each source, the weight your supervisor gave it (high, moderate, low), and the stated or implied reason.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'if the WISC and the classroom observation pointed opposite ways, which would you trust more for this question — and would that change for a different question?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — writing a proposed weighting for each source with one line of reasoning beside it: how reliable, how close to the referral concern, how representative.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the final weighting and any sentence in the report that ranks one informant's account below another's.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — writing reasons makes your implicit weighting visible so it can be corrected. Ranking people's accounts in print has consequences for relationships you do not yet hold.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — state your weighting in supervision before you write the synthesis, and defend it when challenged.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — weighting by status (the psychologist's test over the parent's account) or by convenience (the source that fits your hypothesis) instead of by fit to the question.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'the referral question is about the yard. Which of your sources were actually in the yard?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — decide the weighting, write it into the synthesis in one or two sentences, and bring it to supervision already written.\n\nPASS TEST — The weighting is defensible and written down\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the sentence in the report stating which evidence carried most weight and why. Log against 1. Assessment.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a conclusion that clearly rests on one source, with nothing in the report saying so, so the reader cannot tell what would change it.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain the criteria for weighting (reliability, validity for the specific question, setting, recency, independence from other sources) and give a worked case where the same source deserves high weight for one question and low weight for another.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when new information arrives that overturns your weighting: say so in an addendum rather than quietly revising the conclusion.",
"error": "Letting the standardised score overrule everything else because it has a number attached, without asking whether it answers the referral question.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Write an integration section rather than a list', into Formulation, and into defending conclusions at a review meeting.",
"evidence":"Report — integration and conclusions · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment"},

("Synthesise findings from multiple sources into one profile","Write an integration section rather than a list"): {
"why":   "A report that lists findings test by test hands the hardest part of the job to the reader. Teachers and parents will not integrate a WISC section, a rating-scale section and an observation section on their own; they will skim to the recommendations. Report-writing texts are consistent on this: organise by theme or domain, not by instrument (Lichtenberger, Mather, Kaufman & Kaufman, Essentials of Assessment Report Writing, 2004). An unintegrated report is a common reason for a trainee's report to be sent back.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor's reports are structured. Look for a section headed something like 'integration', 'summary of findings' or 'profile', and see whether it is organised by instrument or by question.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the headings of three of their reports, and one integration paragraph you think works, with why.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'when you write the summary, do you start from the tests or from the referral question?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — drafting the integration section for a case your supervisor is leading, organised by referral question or by domain, with each claim tied to named evidence.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the overall conclusions and the final draft that goes to the family.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — drafting integration is where you find out whether you understood the case. Your supervisor carries the conclusions, so an early draft that gets it wrong costs nothing.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — write the integration section for your own case and bring it to supervision before the recommendations are written.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — a list with joining words. 'The WISC showed X. The teacher reported Y. Observation found Z.' is still a list.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'read me the first sentence of each paragraph. Does it make a claim, or name a test?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — write an integration section where each paragraph opens with a claim about the child, then gives the evidence from several sources, then says what it means for the referral question.\n\nPASS TEST — The reader is not left to do the synthesis\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the report. Name this section in your Appendix 5 entry. Log against 1. Assessment.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — recommendations that do not follow from anything the integration section said, because the integration section did not say anything.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can show a peer how to rewrite a test-by-test summary into claim-first paragraphs, and explain why this is what makes recommendations defensible.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when the findings genuinely do not integrate: say so, name the competing readings, and state what further evidence would decide between them.",
"error": "Repeating every score again in the summary instead of saying what the scores mean together.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Ask whether the profile answers the referral question', into Formulation, and into recommendations the school can act on.",
"evidence":"Report — integration section · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment"},

("Synthesise findings from multiple sources into one profile","Ask whether the profile answers the referral question"): {
"why":   "The referral question is the contract. Schools and parents asked something specific, and a thorough report that answers a different question has failed them, however good the psychometrics. It is easy to drift: the assessment finds something interesting and the report follows it. The check is simple and often skipped — reread the question, reread your conclusion, and see whether one answers the other.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — whether your supervisor goes back to the referral form before finalising a report. Some print it and set it beside the draft.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the referral question in the referrer's own words, and the one-sentence answer your supervisor's report gives to it.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'has a report of yours ever answered a different question from the one that was asked? What happened?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — writing the referral question at the top of the draft in the referrer's words, and a one-line answer to it underneath, before anything else is written.\n\nTHEY HOLD — whether the question itself needs to be renegotiated with the school or parent.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — checking the fit is a habit you can build straight away. Going back to a referrer to say the question has changed is a consultation decision.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — at the end of your draft, state aloud in supervision: the question asked, your answer, and which section of the report carries it.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — an answer that is technically there but buried, or an answer to the question you would have preferred they asked.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'if the principal read only your summary, would they know the answer to what they asked?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — before sending any report, check it against the referral question and fix it if it does not answer it. If it cannot answer it, say why in the report.\n\nPASS TEST — You noticed if it did not\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the report, with the referral question stated at the start and answered in the summary. Note in the Log if the question was renegotiated. Log against 1. Assessment.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — the school rings to ask what the report means for their question.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain to a peer how referral questions drift during an assessment, and show the simple check: question, answer, location in the report.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when the assessment shows the original question was the wrong one: answer it anyway, then say plainly what you think the more useful question is and why.",
"error": "Sending a report that is thorough on cognition when the school asked about behaviour in the yard.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into recommendations, into Working with Others — agreeing the question at the start — and into Formulation.",
"evidence":"Report — reason for referral and summary · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment"},

("Synthesise findings from multiple sources into one profile","State what you did not assess and what that limits"): {
"why":   "Every assessment is partial. A report that does not say what was left out invites the reader to assume it was covered — that language was fine because it was not mentioned, or that mood was screened because anxiety was not reported. Stating the limits of your competence and your evidence is a professional obligation (CORU SoP 1.2; PSI Code 2.3.1). Missing limitations are what an external examiner or a complaint will find first.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — where in your supervisor's reports the limitations appear, and how they are worded. Some write a short paragraph; some fold them into each section.\n\nWRITE DOWN — every domain relevant to the referral that was not directly assessed in the case, and whether the report says so.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'how do you decide what goes in the limitations without making the report sound like it achieved nothing?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — drafting the limitations paragraph: what was not assessed, why (time, consent, scope, a test not available, the child's state on the day), and what that means for the conclusions.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the decision about whether a gap is serious enough to change the conclusions or require an onward referral.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — listing gaps is straightforward. Judging whether a gap undermines the conclusion needs experience of what those gaps usually hide.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — write the limitations yourself and discuss in supervision which of them affect the conclusions directly.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — a boilerplate paragraph ('results should be interpreted with caution') that could go in any report and tells the reader nothing about this one.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'caution about what, exactly? Which conclusion would change if you had assessed that?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — every report you write has a specific limitations paragraph naming what was not assessed and which conclusion that affects.\n\nPASS TEST — A limitations paragraph exists\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the limitations paragraph in the report. Log against 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — no limitations paragraph, or a generic one, so the school acts on a conclusion the evidence does not carry.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain why a limitations paragraph protects the child, the reader and you, and show how to make one specific: name the gap, the reason, and the conclusion it qualifies.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when a gap you did not report turns out to matter later: acknowledge it in writing, correct the record, and tell your supervisor before the family hears it from someone else.",
"error": "A generic 'interpret with caution' line that names no gap and qualifies no conclusion.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into Ethical Practice — working within competence — and into onward referral decisions.",
"evidence":"Report — limitations · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice"},

# ── 1. ASSESSMENT · Phonological and literacy measures ──

("Phonological and literacy measures","Explain why phonological awareness matters for reading"): {
"why":   "Teachers will ask why you tested sound games when the concern was reading. The answer is the core of the literacy evidence base. The Simple View of Reading splits reading into decoding and language comprehension (Gough & Tunmer, Remedial and Special Education, 1986). Phonological processing difficulty is the best-supported account of why decoding fails (the 'phonological core' view — Stanovich, Journal of Learning Disabilities, 1988). If you cannot explain this plainly, the recommendations that follow will not be implemented.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor explains phonological awareness to a teacher or parent. Note the example they use; most use rhyme, syllables and a spoken word broken into sounds.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the explanation in their words, and any question the listener asked that showed confusion.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'how do you explain the difference between phonological awareness and phonics to a teacher who uses the words interchangeably?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — the explanation of phonological awareness in a feedback meeting, prepared in advance and kept to two minutes.\n\nTHEY HOLD — linking it to this child's scores and to what the school should do.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — the general explanation can be rehearsed and is low-risk. Tying it to a named child's results carries the interpretation, which your supervisor still owns at this stage.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — explain phonological awareness and its link to this child's reading in a feedback meeting, with your supervisor present.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — jargon ('phonemic segmentation deficit') and overclaiming, such as implying phonological difficulty is the whole story when comprehension or vocabulary are also weak.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — a supervisor asking, in the meeting, 'could you give an example of what that looks like in class?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — explain it to a teacher or parent in plain language, with one example drawn from this child's work, and check that they understood.\n\nPASS TEST — You can explain it to a teacher without notes\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — a case note of the feedback meeting. Log against 1. Assessment and 5. Working with Others.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — the teacher leaves thinking the answer is more phonics worksheets, or thinking the child cannot learn to read.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain the Simple View of Reading, why decoding and comprehension can fail separately, and where phonological awareness sits — citing sources, not from memory of a lecture.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when a teacher disagrees ('he knows all his sounds'): separate knowing letter–sound links from being able to manipulate sounds in spoken words, and show the difference with a quick oral example.",
"error": "Explaining phonological awareness with the child's scores instead of with an example the teacher will recognise in class.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Distinguish a specific difficulty from a general one', 'Interpret a phonological profile in context', and 'Link the profile to what the school can deliver'.",
"evidence":"Case note of feedback meeting · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment and 5. Working with Others"},

("Phonological and literacy measures","Distinguish a specific difficulty from a general one"): {
"why":   "A child with weak reading and weak general language or reasoning needs different support from a child whose reading difficulty stands apart from otherwise typical abilities. Current definitions of dyslexia treat it as a continuum and do not rely on an IQ–achievement discrepancy (Rose, Identifying and Teaching Children and Young People with Dyslexia and Literacy Difficulties, 2009). But you still have to know the broader profile — especially oral language — before calling the difficulty specific. Skip that and the recommendation targets the wrong thing.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — which information your supervisor looks at before saying a literacy difficulty is 'specific': cognitive scores, oral language, attendance, teaching history, vision and hearing.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the checks they made and any they said were missing.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what would make you say this is a general learning difficulty showing up in reading, rather than a reading difficulty?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — gathering the wider profile: cognitive or language scores, school history, attendance, sensory screening, and whether the child has had adequate teaching.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the conclusion about whether the difficulty is specific, and how it is worded.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — collecting the context teaches you what the question depends on. The label, or the absence of one, is what the family and school will act on.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — present your view in supervision: specific, general, or unclear, and the evidence for each.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — jumping from low phonological scores to 'dyslexic profile' without checking oral language comprehension or response to teaching.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'what's their receptive language like? And how long have they had structured literacy teaching?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — check the broader profile before concluding, and state in the report what that check found.\n\nPASS TEST — You check the cognitive profile before concluding\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the interpretation section of the report, with the broader profile referenced. Log against 1. Assessment.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a report naming a specific literacy difficulty for a child whose oral language is as weak as their reading, so the language need goes unaddressed.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain to a peer why discrepancy definitions were abandoned, what replaced them, and why the wider profile still matters — citing Rose (2009) and your service's own criteria.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when the profile is mixed: describe both needs, say which is primary for the referral question, and avoid a label the evidence does not support.",
"error": "Concluding 'specific' from low phonological scores alone, without checking oral language or teaching history.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Interpret a phonological profile in context', into Formulation, and into Conditions — DLD and literacy difficulty.",
"evidence":"Report — interpretation · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment"},

("Phonological and literacy measures","Administer phonological awareness subtests to standard"): {
"why":   "Phonological tasks are presented orally, so the stimulus is your voice. Speak too slowly, stress the wrong sound or repeat an item not allowed to be repeated, and the score reflects your delivery, not the child's skill. Many batteries use recorded audio for some tasks for this reason: CTOPP-2 (Wagner, Torgesen, Rashotte & Pearson, 2013) supplies audio for some subtests. Check your edition's manual for which ones. An inaccurate administration cannot be rescued at scoring.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor delivers oral items: pace, whether they use the audio where the manual requires it, how they handle a request to repeat, and what they do after a discontinue.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the subtests used, the start points, repeat rules and discontinue rules, checked against the manual afterwards.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what's the easiest way to spoil an elision item without noticing?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — administering one phonological subtest while your supervisor watches, having practised it aloud at least twice beforehand.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the rest of the battery and the decision about whether your administration was valid enough to score.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — oral delivery is a motor skill that needs practice under observation. Whether a spoiled item invalidates the subtest is a judgement you have not yet seen enough to make.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — administer the full phonological battery with your supervisor present.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — adding sounds (saying 'buh' for /b/), unscripted feedback, repeating items the manual says cannot be repeated, and losing track of the discontinue rule.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — a quiet note to check the discontinue rule, or taking over for one item and handing back.",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — administer the battery to manual standard, with the audio where required, correct start points and discontinues, and responses recorded as you go.\n\nPASS TEST — Administration is fluent and timing accurate\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the completed record form. Log against 1. Assessment and Table 3 tools.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a discontinued subtest continued, or items repeated against the rules, so the scaled score cannot be interpreted.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can demonstrate clean phoneme production (no added schwa), explain the start, repeat and discontinue rules from memory, and explain why each rule exists.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you realise mid-battery you broke a rule: note it on the form at that point, decide with the manual whether the subtest is still scoreable, and report the problem if it is not.",
"error": "Adding a vowel to isolated consonants ('buh' for /b/), which changes the task the child is doing.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Score the phonological composites correctly' and 'Interpret a phonological profile in context'.",
"evidence":"Completed protocol · Appendix 7 if observed · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment"},

("Phonological and literacy measures","Administer rapid naming tasks accurately"): {
"why":   "Rapid automatised naming (RAN) — naming arrays of digits, letters, colours or objects as fast as possible — predicts reading fluency partly independently of phonological awareness. This is the basis of the double-deficit hypothesis (Wolf & Bowers, Journal of Educational Psychology, 1999). The score is a time, often adjusted for errors, so a stopwatch started late or an error not recorded changes the result directly.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — exactly when your supervisor starts and stops the timer, how they record errors and self-corrections, and whether they use the practice items as the manual requires.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the timing rule (when the clock starts and stops), how errors are counted, and whether the test has an error cut-off that invalidates a trial.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what do you do if a child loses their place on the array?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — timing and error recording while your supervisor administers, then compare your times with theirs.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the administration and the decision on which timing counts if yours differs.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — timing reliability is checkable against another person, so it is the right thing to practise first. Administration adds the load of instructions and managing the child.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — administer and time the RAN tasks with your supervisor present, timing in parallel.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — timer starts that lag the child's first response, errors missed because you were watching the clock, and prompts the manual does not allow.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — comparing the two times afterwards and asking why they differ by more than a second.",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — administer, time and record errors to manual standard, and note anything that affected speed (stammer, naming difficulty, losing place).\n\nPASS TEST — You timed and recorded errors correctly\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the completed record form with times and errors. Log against 1. Assessment.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a time that includes your instructions, or no record of errors, so the score and any error adjustment are wrong.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain what RAN measures and does not measure, why it relates to fluency, and how to time it reliably — with the double-deficit literature cited.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when the timing is compromised (interruption, timer failure): note it, do not estimate, and check the manual on whether the trial can be re-run.",
"error": "Starting the timer on your own instruction rather than on the child's first response, which adds seconds to every trial.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Score the phonological composites correctly' and 'Interpret a phonological profile in context' — especially fluency-focused recommendations.",
"evidence":"Completed protocol with times · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment"},

("Phonological and literacy measures","Score the phonological composites correctly"): {
"why":   "Composite scores are what reports quote and what schools act on, and they are built from several conversions: raw to scaled for each subtest, scaled summed to a composite, composite to percentile and confidence interval. Each step uses the child's exact age on the day. On CTOPP-2 (Wagner et al., 2013), subtests are scaled scores (mean 10, SD 3) and composites are index scores (mean 100, SD 15). Which subtests feed which composite changes with age band, so check the manual for your edition. One wrong table and the whole profile shifts.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor calculates chronological age, which norm table they turn to, and whether they double-check the sum of scaled scores before looking up the composite.\n\nWRITE DOWN — for each composite, which subtests feed it at this child's age, and the table used.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'which scoring mistake have you seen most often in trainee protocols?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — scoring the protocol independently, from raw scores to composites, before seeing your supervisor's scoring.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the authoritative score, and resolving any difference between your scoring and theirs.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — independent double-scoring is the fastest way to find your systematic errors. Your supervisor's version stands until you have shown you score accurately.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — score the protocol and the composites yourself; your supervisor checks it before anything is reported.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — age calculated wrongly (especially borrowing months and days), the wrong age-band table, a subtest summed into the wrong composite, and transcription errors from protocol to report.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — handing it back with 'one of these is wrong — find it.'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — score, then check every step a second time on a different day or with scoring software, and reconcile any difference before reporting.\n\nPASS TEST — Composites calculated and checked\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the scored protocol with your check marked, and the score table in the report. Log against 1. Assessment.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a composite in the report that does not match the protocol, found by the family's private assessor or at a review.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can walk a peer through age calculation, subtest-to-composite mapping, and confidence intervals, and explain why a composite is more reliable than any single subtest.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you find a scoring error after the report has gone: tell your supervisor, issue a corrected report with the change marked, and tell the family and school what changed and whether the conclusion changes.",
"error": "Calculating age without borrowing correctly across months and days, and reading off the wrong norm table.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Interpret a phonological profile in context' and into the score table and limitations of the report.",
"evidence":"Scored protocol with check · Report — score table · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment"},

("Phonological and literacy measures","Interpret a phonological profile in context"): {
"why":   "A low phonological composite shows the child did poorly on these tasks, on this day, compared with the norm sample. It does not by itself establish dyslexia, explain every reading problem, or predict what intervention will work. Interpretation means putting the scores beside reading accuracy, fluency, comprehension, oral language, teaching history and the child's own account. Rose (2009) is explicit that dyslexia is best thought of as a continuum and that response to intervention is part of the picture. Overclaim here and the family leaves with a label the evidence does not carry.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — the words your supervisor uses when interpreting phonological scores: 'consistent with', 'suggests', 'is characteristic of'. Note what they will not say.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the other evidence they set beside the scores, and any conclusion they chose not to draw.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what would you need to see before you'd say this profile shows a dyslexic-type difficulty?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — drafting the interpretation paragraph: what the scores show, which other evidence agrees or disagrees, and the limits.\n\nTHEY HOLD — any diagnostic or quasi-diagnostic language, and how the conclusion is framed for the family.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — writing interpretation shows your reasoning. Diagnostic language has consequences for school supports and for the child's view of themselves, so it stays with them.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — write the interpretation and present it in supervision, including one sentence on what the profile does not establish.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — reading scores as causes ('because of her phonological deficit') and ignoring evidence of adequate teaching, attendance, or language.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'you've said this explains her reading. What else could explain it?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — interpret the profile against the wider evidence, and write explicitly what it does and does not establish.\n\nPASS TEST — You can say what it does and does not establish\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the interpretation and limitations sections of the report. Log against 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — 'the results confirm dyslexia' on the basis of one battery, with no reference to the child's response to teaching.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain the difference between describing a profile and diagnosing, why phonological scores are one strand of evidence, and how to write both halves: what it shows and what it does not.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when a parent wants a label the evidence does not support: explain what the evidence does show, what support follows from it without a label, and what would need to happen for the question to be revisited.",
"error": "Writing that the scores 'confirm' a difficulty rather than that they are consistent with it alongside the other evidence.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Link the profile to what the school can deliver', into Formulation, and into Conditions — literacy difficulty and DLD.",
"evidence":"Report — interpretation and limitations · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Phonological and literacy measures","Link the profile to what the school can deliver"): {
"why":   "A recommendation the school cannot deliver is not a recommendation. Irish primary schools allocate special education teaching under the model set out in DES Circular 0013/2017 (post-primary 0014/2017), within the NEPS Continuum of Support. What is possible depends on staffing, timetable and the programmes staff are trained in. Recommendations should follow from the finding — phonological weakness points towards explicit, systematic phonological and phonics teaching — and be specific enough to act on.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor finds out what the school already offers before recommending. Listen for questions about the special education team, programmes in use, group sizes and timetabling.\n\nWRITE DOWN — each recommendation in their report, the finding it follows from, and whether it names who, what, how often and for how long.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'how do you write a recommendation that is honest about what the child needs when the school can't provide all of it?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — drafting the recommendations for the literacy section, each linked in one line to the finding it comes from.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the conversation with the school about what is feasible, and the final wording.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — linking finding to recommendation is reasoning you can practise. Negotiating provision with a principal involves a relationship and service expectations you do not yet hold.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — ask the school what it can offer, write recommendations that fit, and present them in the feedback meeting with your supervisor there.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — generic recommendations ('multisensory approach', 'extra time') that do not follow from this child's profile, and naming a programme the school does not have or cannot resource.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'which finding does that recommendation come from?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — write recommendations that each follow from a named finding and that the school has confirmed it can deliver or adapt.\n\nPASS TEST — The recommendation follows from the finding\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the recommendations section and the case note of the school conversation. Log against 1. Assessment, 3. Intervention and 4. Systemic Change.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — ten recommendations copied from a template, none implemented, and a review six months later with nothing changed.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain how the Continuum of Support and the allocation model shape what schools can do, and how to write a recommendation that names what, who, how often, for how long and how progress will be measured.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when the child needs more than the school can give: state the need honestly, recommend what is feasible now, and say who else should be involved.",
"error": "A list of generic literacy recommendations that could have gone in any child's report.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into Intervention — planning and review — and into Systemic Change, where repeated gaps in school provision become a consultation issue.",
"evidence":"Report — recommendations · case note · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment and 3. Intervention"},

# ── 1. ASSESSMENT · Social communication screening measures ──

("Social communication screening measures","Recognise the limits of a screening instrument"): {
"why":   "A screen finds people who may need a fuller assessment. It does not diagnose. Questionnaires such as the Social Communication Questionnaire (SCQ; Rutter, Bailey & Lord, 2003) or the Social Responsiveness Scale (SRS-2; Constantino & Gruber, 2012) rely on one informant's view and use cut-offs chosen to trade missed cases against false alarms. Sensitivity and specificity vary by sample and age — check the manual and do not quote a figure from memory. A family who hears 'the score was high' as 'your child is autistic' has been misled.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor describes a screening result, in the report and aloud. Notice the words they avoid.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the instrument, the informant, the cut-off, what the manual says the cut-off means, and the exact sentence your supervisor used to report it.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'have you ever had a screen come back below the cut-off for a child you were sure needed a full assessment? What did you do?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — drafting the paragraph that reports the screen, including a sentence saying what a screen can and cannot establish.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the recommendation about onward referral, and the conversation with the family.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — the wording can be practised and corrected on paper. The onward decision and the family conversation carry emotional weight and service consequences.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — report the screen yourself, in writing and aloud to the family, with your supervisor present.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — diagnostic language ('scores in the autistic range') and false reassurance from a low score when observation or history raise concern.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — your supervisor adding, 'just to be clear, this is a questionnaire, not an assessment for autism.'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — report every screen with a written statement that it is not a diagnosis, what it does suggest, and what happens next.\n\nPASS TEST — You said in writing that a screen is not a diagnosis\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the report paragraph. Log against 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a school telling a parent 'the psychologist said he's autistic' because your report said 'scores in the autism range'.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain sensitivity, specificity and why cut-offs are compromises, and why a single-informant questionnaire cannot carry a diagnosis.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when a screen is being used as a diagnosis by the school or family: correct it in writing, explain the difference, and point to the pathway.",
"error": "Writing 'in the autism range' instead of 'above the screening threshold, which indicates a fuller assessment is warranted'.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Know the onward diagnostic pathway in this service', 'Integrate screening data with direct observation', and Ethical Practice.",
"evidence":"Report — findings and limitations · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Social communication screening measures","Know the onward diagnostic pathway in this service"): {
"why":   "A positive screen with nowhere to go leaves the family holding a worry and no route. In Ireland, autism diagnostic assessment is usually done by HSE multidisciplinary teams — Children's Disability Network Teams, primary care, or CAMHS where there is a co-occurring mental health need — or by private assessment. Assessment of Need under the Disability Act 2005 is one statutory route. Routes, waiting times and who can refer vary by area and change often, so find out the current local position rather than assuming.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — what your supervisor tells a family after a positive screen: who they should contact, who makes the referral, and roughly how long it takes.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the local pathway as your supervisor describes it: team names, referral route, who can refer, whether the school or GP is involved, and any local waiting time they quote.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what's changed in this pathway in the last two years, and where do you check it's still current?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — writing a one-page summary of the local pathway for your own use, and checking it with your supervisor.\n\nTHEY HOLD — advising the family on the route, and any referral made.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — mapping the pathway is research you can do alone and it builds your knowledge of the area. Advice to a family must be current and accurate, so your supervisor gives it until you have checked the map.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — explain the pathway to a family after a screen, with your supervisor there.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — out-of-date information, promises about timescales, and not mentioning interim support the school can put in place without a diagnosis.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — your supervisor adding what the school can do in the meantime.",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — name the onward pathway in the report and to the family, accurately for the area, with interim supports alongside.\n\nPASS TEST — You could name where a positive screen goes\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the recommendations section of the report and the pathway summary on file. Log against 1. Assessment and 5. Working with Others.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — 'the family should seek a diagnostic assessment', with no route named, and a family that does not know where to start.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain the Irish routes (HSE teams, Assessment of Need, CAMHS, private), who refers, and the difference between a need-led support in school and a diagnosis-led service.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when the pathway is blocked or very long: say so honestly, recommend what the school can do now, and record the gap for your service's systemic view.",
"error": "Telling a family to 'seek a diagnosis' without naming who, how, or what to do while they wait.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into Working with Others — interagency referral — and into Systemic Change, where pathway gaps are raised.",
"evidence":"Report — recommendations · pathway summary on file · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment and 5. Working with Others"},

("Social communication screening measures","Administer a screen with a parent"): {
"why":   "Parents complete screening questionnaires about their own child, often while already worried or defending against a worry. How you introduce it shapes what they report. Some over-endorse to secure help; some under-endorse to protect the child from a label. Explaining what the screen is for, what it cannot tell them, and what happens next is part of informed consent. It also makes the answers more accurate.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor introduces the questionnaire: purpose, how long it takes, how to handle items they are unsure of, and who will see the results.\n\nWRITE DOWN — their introduction as close to verbatim as you can, and any question the parent asked.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what do you say when a parent asks, before they start, whether a high score means autism?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — introducing the questionnaire to the parent, from a script you have written and checked with your supervisor.\n\nTHEY HOLD — questions about diagnosis, and any distress that comes up.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — a scripted introduction is safe to practise. Questions about what the child 'has' need someone who can answer them in the moment without overstating.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — introduce the questionnaire, answer the parent's questions, and stay available while they complete it, with your supervisor present.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — leading the parent ('does he do this a lot?') or interpreting items in a way that shifts the answer.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — your supervisor offering the standard response to an unclear item: 'answer as best you can and we'll talk about it after.'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — introduce the screen, explain what it can and cannot tell them, give neutral help with unclear items, and tell them when and how they will hear the results.\n\nPASS TEST — You explained what a screen can and cannot tell them\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — a case note recording the explanation given. Log against 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a parent who completed the form thinking the score would give them a diagnosis, and who is distressed or angry when it does not.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain why the introduction matters for validity as well as consent, and how to answer a parent's questions about an item without shifting their answer.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when a parent becomes distressed or angry during the questionnaire: stop, acknowledge it, offer to finish another time, and record that the conditions may have affected the responses.",
"error": "Handing the questionnaire over without explaining that it is a screen and not a diagnosis.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Integrate screening data with direct observation', into consent practice in Ethical Practice, and into Working with Others — families.",
"evidence":"Case note · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Social communication screening measures","Integrate screening data with direct observation"): {
"why":   "A questionnaire records one informant's summary of the child. Observation shows you the child — in the yard, in class, with peers and adults. Each can mislead alone. The screen can be skewed by the informant, and a short observation can catch an untypical day. Guidance on autism assessment consistently requires information from more than one source and setting — check the specific requirements of the guidance your service uses. Relying on the questionnaire alone makes the screen do a job it was not built for.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — which settings your supervisor chooses to observe after a screen, and what they look for: initiation, response to bids, reciprocity, play, transitions, sensory response.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the observation focus, the setting, and how the observation was later set beside the questionnaire.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'when the screen and your observation disagree, what do you do?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — the observation in one setting, with a focus agreed beforehand from the screen items.\n\nTHEY HOLD — putting the two together and deciding what it means for onward referral.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — a focused observation is a skill you are already building. Integrating it with a screen and deciding on referral is a clinical judgement.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — plan the observation from the screen, observe, and present the integration in supervision.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — observing only structured class time when the concern is unstructured social time, and treating 'did not see it' as 'it does not happen'.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'the parent reported difficulty at break. When did you observe?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — observe in at least one setting chosen to test what the screen suggested, and write a paragraph that sets the two sources together.\n\nPASS TEST — You did not rely on the questionnaire alone\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the observation write-up and the integration paragraph in the report. Log against 1. Assessment.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a report whose conclusions rest entirely on a questionnaire score.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain why screening and observation are complementary, how to choose an observation setting from screen items, and why absence of a behaviour in one short observation is weak evidence.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when the two sources flatly contradict: report both, give the likeliest reasons, and let that uncertainty inform the onward referral rather than hiding it.",
"error": "Observing in the setting that was easiest to arrange rather than the one where the concern was reported.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Recognise masked or atypical presentation', into 'Identify where sources diverge and say so', and into onward referral.",
"evidence":"Observation write-up · Report — integration · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment"},

("Social communication screening measures","Recognise masked or atypical presentation"): {
"why":   "Some autistic children — often, but not only, girls — learn to hide or compensate for social differences, at great cost to themselves. This is camouflaging, described in Hull et al. (Journal of Autism and Developmental Disorders, 2017). A meta-analysis estimated the true male-to-female ratio nearer 3:1 than the 4:1 often quoted, pointing to under-identification of girls (Loomes, Hull & Mandy, JAACAP, 2017). Screens and short observations may miss these children. The PSI Autism Guidelines (2022) are the reference named for this standard — read them directly rather than relying on a summary.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor asks about effort and exhaustion as well as behaviour: after-school collapse, scripted conversation, intense friendships that break down, anxiety, masking at school and releasing at home.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the questions they ask that go beyond the screen items.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what makes you suspect camouflaging when school says everything is fine?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — preparing a short list of questions on masking and effort for the parent interview and the child, drawn from the literature, and checking them with your supervisor.\n\nTHEY HOLD — interpreting the answers and deciding whether they warrant onward referral despite a sub-threshold screen.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — the questions are the part you can research and improve. Acting on a hunch against a sub-threshold score needs experience of how often that hunch is right.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — ask about masking in interview, with your supervisor present, and bring your view to supervision.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — dismissing home reports because school sees no difficulty, and assuming a girl who makes eye contact and has friends cannot be autistic.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'what happens when she gets home from school?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — consider camouflaging and late identification in every social communication assessment, and write in the report that you did.\n\nPASS TEST — You considered presentation in girls and late identification\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — a sentence in the report on how presentation and masking were considered. Log against 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a sub-threshold screen and a calm classroom observation reported as 'no social communication concerns', and a teenager identified years later.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain camouflaging, why screens under-identify it, and what questions surface it — citing Hull et al. (2017), Loomes et al. (2017) and the PSI guidelines.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when home and school accounts are irreconcilable: treat the difference as a possible sign of masking, not a reason to doubt the parent.",
"error": "Taking a calm classroom and a sub-threshold screen as evidence against autism in a child who is exhausted and distressed at home.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Analyse informant agreement and discrepancy', into Conditions — autism, and into Formulation.",
"evidence":"Report — findings and limitations · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice"},

# ── 1. ASSESSMENT · Behavioural and emotional rating scales ──

("Behavioural and emotional rating scales","Select the right forms for informant and age"): {
"why":   "Rating scale systems come as families of forms for different informants and age bands. Using the wrong one gives you scores compared against the wrong norm group. The BASC-3 (Reynolds & Kamphaus, 2015) has teacher, parent and self-report forms by age band. The ASEBA (Achenbach & Rescorla, 2001) has the CBCL, TRF and Youth Self-Report (YSR). The SDQ (Goodman, 1997) has parent, teacher and self-report versions, the self-report for ages 11–17. Check the exact age ranges in the manual you are using before choosing.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor decides which forms go to whom: the child's age, which adults know the child in which setting, and whether the child can self-report.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the instrument, each form used, who completed it, and the age range printed on each form.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'which teacher do you send it to when a post-primary student has eight?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — proposing the forms, informants and age bands for the case, in writing, before anything is sent.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the final choice of instrument and of informants.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — form selection is rule-based and easy to check, so it is the right thing to practise. Choosing the instrument depends on the referral question and on what the service holds.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — select and send the forms yourself; your supervisor checks your selection before they go out.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — a form one band too young or too old for the child, a teacher who barely knows the child, and no self-report where one is available and appropriate.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'she turns twelve next month — which form will you use, and why?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — choose forms matched to the age band on the date of completion, send them to informants who know the child in the relevant setting, and record who completed each.\n\nPASS TEST — Parent, teacher and self-report chosen appropriately\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the method section of the report naming each form and informant. Log against 1. Assessment.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — scores reported from a form normed for the wrong age band, found when someone checks the date of birth.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain the structure of at least two rating scale systems, how to match forms to age and informant, and why the choice of informant is part of the evidence.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when the wrong form was completed: do not score it against the wrong norms. Report it as descriptive only, or re-send the right form.",
"error": "Using a form normed for the wrong age band because the child's birthday fell between sending and completion.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Include the child's own self-report where age-appropriate', 'Score and convert to standardised scores', and 'Analyse informant agreement and discrepancy'.",
"evidence":"Report — method · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment"},

("Behavioural and emotional rating scales","Include the child's own self-report where age-appropriate"): {
"why":   "Children have a right to express their views in matters affecting them, and those views must be given due weight (UNCRC Article 12). In assessment, that means the child's own account sits beside the adults' — not below it, and not only where it agrees. Internal states such as anxiety and low mood are often under-reported by adults who see only behaviour. Lundy's model of participation — space, voice, audience, influence (British Educational Research Journal, 2007) — is a useful check that the view was heard and acted on.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor introduces a self-report form to a child: who reads the items, how they check understanding, and whether the child is told who will see the answers.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the form, the child's age, how it was administered, and where the child's view appeared in the report.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what do you do when a child's self-report says everything is fine and every adult says it isn't?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — administering the self-report with the child, reading items aloud where reading is a barrier, and recording anything the child says about the items.\n\nTHEY HOLD — how the child's account is weighted and worded in the report.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — sitting with the child is direct, supervised practice. Where their view lands in the report shapes the conclusions, which your supervisor still holds.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — administer the self-report and write the section presenting the child's view, with your supervisor reviewing before it goes in.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — the child's view summarised in one line after pages of adult ratings, or dismissed as 'lacking insight' without evidence.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'where in the report would the child find what they told you?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — include self-report wherever the child's age and ability allow, present it alongside adult reports, and say how it informed the conclusions.\n\nPASS TEST — The child's account appears alongside the adults'\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the findings section showing the child's account in parallel with adult ratings. Log against 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a report on a thirteen-year-old's anxiety with no self-report and no direct quote from the young person.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain Article 12, Lundy's model, and the practical adaptations — reading items aloud, visual scales, checking understanding — that let younger or less able children self-report validly.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when the child refuses or cannot complete a self-report: record that, and gather their view another way — conversation, drawing, scaling questions.",
"error": "Leaving out the child's own view because the adults' forms were enough to score.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Analyse informant agreement and discrepancy', into Ethical Practice — the child's voice — and into Working with Others.",
"evidence":"Report — findings · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Behavioural and emotional rating scales","Explain to informants how to complete them"): {
"why":   "A rating scale is only as good as the completion. Forms come back with half the items blank, several boxes ticked on one item, notes in the margins instead of ratings, or completed jointly by two parents without saying so. Each invalidates or weakens the score. Most manuals set a maximum number of omitted items before a scale cannot be scored — check yours. Two minutes of explanation at the start saves a wasted form and a second request to a busy teacher.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — what your supervisor says when handing out or sending forms: the time period to rate, answering every item, one rater per form, what to do when unsure, and the return date.\n\nWRITE DOWN — their instructions, and anything they put in a covering note for teachers.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what's the most common way forms come back unusable?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — writing a covering note for teacher forms and giving the verbal explanation to parents, checked beforehand by your supervisor.\n\nTHEY HOLD — deciding whether a returned form is valid enough to score.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — the explanation is a script you can improve with feedback. Judging validity depends on the manual's rules and on experience of how different errors affect scores.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — explain the forms to every informant yourself, and check the forms on return before the informant leaves where you can.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — not stating the rating period ('in the last six months'), and not asking the informant to answer every item.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'did you mention the time period? Some of these answers sound like last year.'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — explain the rating period, one rater per form, answering every item, and how to handle uncertainty. Check forms on return and follow up omissions quickly.\n\nPASS TEST — Forms come back usable\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the covering note template and returned forms. Log against 1. Assessment and 5. Working with Others.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a teacher form with twelve items blank, discovered a week later when the teacher has moved on to other work.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain why each instruction matters for the score, and write a short covering note that gets forms back complete.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when a form comes back incomplete: check the manual's omission rule, follow up the specific items if it is over, and never fill in or pro-rate beyond what the manual permits.",
"error": "Sending forms without a stated rating period and a request to answer every item.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Score and convert to standardised scores' and into Working with Others — teachers and families as informants.",
"evidence":"Covering note · returned forms · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment and 5. Working with Others"},

("Behavioural and emotional rating scales","Score and convert to standardised scores"): {
"why":   "Most behaviour rating systems convert raw scores to T-scores (mean 50, SD 10) against a norm group chosen by age, sometimes sex, and sometimes a general or clinical sample. Choosing the wrong norm group, missing reverse-scored items, or mistyping into software all change the result without any sign on the page. Hand-scoring invites arithmetic errors; software moves the error to data entry. Either way, what makes the score trustworthy is the check.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — whether your supervisor hand-scores or uses software, which norm group they choose and why, and how they check the entry.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the instrument, the norm group, how omitted items were handled, and any validity or response-style index reported.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'when do you use combined-sex norms and when separate, and does it change what you conclude?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — scoring the forms independently, including any validity indices, before seeing your supervisor's version.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the authoritative score and the choice of norm group.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — double-scoring shows your systematic errors quickly. Norm-group choice changes the conclusion, so it is a judgement they keep until you can defend it.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — score, choose norms, and present the scores in supervision with the norm group stated.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — reverse-scored items missed, the wrong age band, validity indices ignored, and scores typed into the report wrongly.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — picking one scale and asking you to re-score it by hand in front of them.",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — score each form, check the scoring by a second method or on a second occasion, state the norm group in the report, and check validity indices before interpreting.\n\nPASS TEST — Scoring is checked\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — scored forms or software printout with your check marked, and the score table in the report. Log against 1. Assessment.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a clinically significant score reported from a form whose validity index flagged an extreme response style.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain T-scores, norm-group choice, reverse scoring and validity indices, and show a peer a quick way to check software output against the raw form.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when a validity index is flagged: do not interpret the clinical scales as if it were not, report the flag, and consider what the response style itself tells you.",
"error": "Interpreting clinical scales without first checking the form's validity or response-style indices.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Analyse informant agreement and discrepancy' and 'Distinguish elevated from clinically significant'.",
"evidence":"Scored forms with check · Report — score table · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment"},

("Behavioural and emotional rating scales","Analyse informant agreement and discrepancy"): {
"why":   "Parent, teacher and child ratings routinely disagree. The Achenbach, McConaughy and Howell (1987) meta-analysis found cross-informant correlations were modest, and higher between similar informants than between different types — check the paper for the figures before quoting. Later work argues discrepancy often reflects real differences in how the child behaves across settings (De Los Reyes et al., Psychological Bulletin, 2015, on the Operations Triad Model). Treating discrepancy as someone being wrong throws away information about context.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor lays out scores from different informants: usually side by side by scale. Notice what they say about a scale where home and school differ sharply.\n\nWRITE DOWN — each scale where informants differ by a meaningful amount (your manual may give a guide), and the explanation your supervisor offered.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'when a parent rates much higher than the teacher, what are the possible explanations, and how do you choose?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — building the side-by-side table and listing, for each discrepancy, explanations based on setting, task demands, relationship and informant threshold.\n\nTHEY HOLD — which explanation the report adopts, and how it is worded.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — generating alternative explanations is safe practice. The wording can make a parent feel disbelieved or a teacher feel blamed.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — present the discrepancies and your reading of them in supervision, then draft the paragraph.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — averaging across informants, or writing one informant off as biased without evidence.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'what's different about the two settings that could make both ratings accurate?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — lay out informant scores side by side, treat each discrepancy as a question about context, and write the likeliest explanation with its evidence.\n\nPASS TEST — Discrepancy treated as data about context\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the rating scale section of the report. Log against 1. Assessment and 5. Working with Others.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — 'the parent's ratings may reflect heightened anxiety', written with no evidence and read by the parent at the feedback meeting.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain why informants disagree, cite the cross-informant literature, and show a peer how to turn a discrepancy into a hypothesis about context that feeds the formulation.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when an informant disputes your reading of their rating: listen, check your interpretation against what they say about the setting, and revise if they have told you something you did not know.",
"error": "Averaging parent and teacher scores into one number, losing the difference that was the finding.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Identify where sources diverge and say so', into Formulation — context and maintaining factors — and into Working with Others.",
"evidence":"Report — rating scale findings · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment and 5. Working with Others"},

("Behavioural and emotional rating scales","Distinguish elevated from clinically significant"): {
"why":   "Rating systems band T-scores, and the bands mean specific things. On the BASC-3, clinical scales at 60–69 are 'at-risk' and 70 and above 'clinically significant' (Reynolds & Kamphaus, 2015). On the ASEBA syndrome scales, 65–69 is borderline and 70 and above clinical (Achenbach & Rescorla, 2001). Other instruments use other cut-offs, so check each manual. 'Clinically significant' is a statistical statement about rarity against norms, not a diagnosis. Misusing either term misleads the reader about how concerned to be.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — the exact words your supervisor uses for each band, and whether they explain the band in plain language in the report.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the instrument, its band labels and cut-offs as printed in the manual, and the sentence your supervisor used to report an elevated score.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'how do you explain to a parent that a clinically significant score doesn't mean their child has a disorder?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — writing the band label and plain-language meaning beside each score in the draft score table.\n\nTHEY HOLD — how the scores are summarised in the conclusion and discussed with the family.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — attaching the right label is checkable against the manual. Deciding how much weight an elevated score carries in the conclusion is interpretation.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — report the rating scale results in writing and in the feedback meeting, with your supervisor present.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — 'clinical' used to mean 'diagnosed', one instrument's cut-offs applied to another, and at-risk scores ignored because they are not clinical.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — your supervisor rephrasing: 'what that means is that only about two in every hundred children in the norm group scored this high.'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — report each score with the manual's band label, what the band means statistically, and a plain-language sentence, without implying diagnosis.\n\nPASS TEST — You know what the cut-offs mean and say so\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the score table and narrative in the report. Log against 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a school acting on 'clinically significant anxiety' as if a diagnosis had been made.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain T-scores, percentiles and band cut-offs, why cut-offs differ between instruments, and why statistical rarity is not the same as disorder.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when a score sits just under a cut-off but everything else suggests serious concern: report the score honestly, and let the wider evidence carry the conclusion.",
"error": "Using 'clinically significant' as if it meant 'diagnosed'.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into interpretation and limitations, into Formulation, and into onward referral decisions.",
"evidence":"Report — score table and narrative · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice"},

# ── 1. ASSESSMENT · Adapt standardised procedure and record the adaptation ──

("Adapt standardised procedure and record the adaptation","Choose an adaptation proportionate to the need"): {
"why":   "Some children cannot access a standardised procedure as written: sensory, motor, language, attention or anxiety needs get in the way. Refusing to adapt can breach the duty of reasonable accommodation (Equal Status Acts 2000–2018). Adapting too much means the score no longer means what the norms say. The Standards for Educational and Psychological Testing (AERA, APA & NCME, 2014) separate accommodations, which leave the construct intact, from modifications, which change it. The skill is choosing the least adaptation that removes the barrier.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — which adaptations your supervisor uses, and when: breaks, a changed order, repeated instructions, a different seating arrangement, an interpreter, or stopping.\n\nWRITE DOWN — each adaptation, the barrier it responded to, and whether your supervisor considered it an accommodation or a modification.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what's the smallest change you could have made there, and why did you choose that one?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — planning adaptations before the session from what you know of the child, and writing down why each is needed.\n\nTHEY HOLD — decisions made in the room, and whether a proposed change would invalidate the norms.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — planning ahead is reasoning you can check against the manual. In-the-moment decisions need experience of how each change affects the score.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — administer with your planned adaptations and make in-session decisions yourself, with your supervisor present.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — over-adapting (rephrasing items, extra prompts) out of kindness, and under-adapting out of fear of breaking the rules.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — suggesting a break rather than a rephrase: 'shall we stop for a minute?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — choose each adaptation deliberately from the least-change options (break, rephrase within the rules, reorder, discontinue), and know which ones change the construct.\n\nPASS TEST — Break, rephrase, reorder or discontinue, chosen deliberately\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the protocol annotations and the report's administration notes. Log against 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a child who was given extra time and rephrased items reported with standard scores and no comment.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain accommodation versus modification with worked examples, the reasonable-accommodation duty, and what each common adaptation does to interpretability.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when an adaptation turns out to have changed the construct: report scores as descriptive rather than normative, and say why.",
"error": "Rephrasing items to help the child, then reporting standard scores as though nothing changed.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'State the effect on validity in the report', 'Record the adaptation at the moment it happens', and Ethical Practice.",
"evidence":"Annotated protocol · Report — administration notes · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Adapt standardised procedure and record the adaptation","State the effect on validity in the report"): {
"why":   "Norms describe how children performed under standard conditions. Once conditions change, the reader needs to know whether the comparison still holds. Test publishers' guidance is that non-standard administration must be reported with its likely effect (AERA, APA & NCME, 2014; PSI Code 4.2.2). A score reported without that note tells the reader it means the same as any other score. If it does not, the report has misled them.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — where your supervisor reports adaptations: usually a short 'administration and validity' or 'behaviour during assessment' paragraph. Notice whether they say which scores are affected.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the adaptation, the subtests affected, and the sentence stating whether scores remain interpretable.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'when do you decide a score can't be reported at all?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — drafting the validity paragraph, naming each adaptation and the subtests it touched.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the judgement on whether the affected scores are interpretable, descriptive only, or should be withheld.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — listing what changed is a record-keeping task. Whether a score still means something requires knowledge of the test's construct and norms.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — write the validity paragraph with your own judgement on interpretability, and defend it in supervision.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — a validity statement that says 'results are a valid estimate' by default, and affected scores left in composites without comment.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'that subtest was timed and you gave extra time. Can it go into the index?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — for every adapted administration, state in the report what changed, which scores it affects, and whether each remains interpretable against the norms.\n\nPASS TEST — You said whether scores remain interpretable\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the administration and validity paragraph of the report. Log against 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — an index score built from a subtest you administered non-standardly, reported without comment and used to decide provision.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain how to judge interpretability subtest by subtest, when to report scores as descriptive, and when to withhold a composite.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you realise after sending the report that a score was compromised: tell your supervisor, issue a correction, and say whether the conclusions change.",
"error": "A validity statement that says the results are valid when the administration was adapted.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Record the adaptation at the moment it happens', into the limitations paragraph, and into Ethical Practice.",
"evidence":"Report — administration and validity · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Adapt standardised procedure and record the adaptation","Recognise when standard procedure is not working"): {
"why":   "Standard procedure stops working in recognisable ways: the child disengages, becomes distressed, cannot understand the instructions, or answers from anxiety rather than ability. The later you notice, the more items are spoiled and the harder the session is to rescue. Early signs are often small — slowing responses, a change in posture, 'I don't know' before the item has been read. Noticing early is what makes a small adaptation enough.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — the moment your supervisor changes something, and what they noticed just before. Often it is a behaviour you did not register.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the sign, the time, the subtest, and what your supervisor did.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what did you see just before you offered a break?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — watching and noting signs of strain during your supervisor's administration, and comparing your notes with theirs afterwards.\n\nTHEY HOLD — deciding when to act.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — when you are not administering, all your attention can go on the child, which is how you learn to see the signs. Deciding to act changes the assessment, so it stays with them.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — administer, watch for signs, and act when you see them, with your supervisor present.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — pressing on to finish the subtest when the child is clearly struggling, because stopping feels like failure.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — a quiet 'how are you doing?' to the child, which gives you the cue.",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — notice strain early and act in proportion, and record when and what you saw.\n\nPASS TEST — You noticed early rather than at the end\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — protocol annotations with time and behaviour, and the behavioural observations section of the report. Log against 1. Assessment.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a child in tears by the final subtest, and a protocol with no note of when it started.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can describe the early signs of disengagement, anxiety and confusion, and how to tell them apart, and explain why acting early protects both the child and the validity of the scores.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you missed the signs and the session ended badly: tell your supervisor, check on the child, record it, and judge whether the later scores can be reported.",
"error": "Pushing through to the end of the subtest because stopping feels like losing the score.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Record the adaptation at the moment it happens', 'Re-evaluate whether to continue at all', and the behavioural observations section.",
"evidence":"Annotated protocol · Report — behavioural observations · Appendix 5 · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment"},

("Adapt standardised procedure and record the adaptation","Record the adaptation at the moment it happens"): {
"why":   "A reconstructed record is not a record. By the time you write up, you will not remember which item you rephrased, how long the break was, or whether you repeated an instruction. CORU SoP 2.8 ties record-keeping to accuracy and timeliness. A protocol annotated in the moment is the only thing that lets you, your supervisor or a later reviewer judge which scores were affected. Without it you cannot write the validity statement honestly.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how and when your supervisor annotates the protocol: margin notes, standard abbreviations, the time written beside a break.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the abbreviations they use and what each means.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what's your shorthand for a repeated instruction, a prompt and a break?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — annotating the protocol in real time while your supervisor administers.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the administration.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — annotating at speed while watching the child is its own skill. Practising it without the load of administering builds the habit before you need both at once.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — administer and annotate at the same time, with your supervisor present.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — adaptations made and not written down, and notes added afterwards from memory.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — at the end, 'show me where you wrote down the break.'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — annotate every adaptation on the protocol as it happens: what changed, which item, the time. Do not add to the record after the session except as a dated, clearly marked addition.\n\nPASS TEST — Annotated on the protocol, not reconstructed\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the annotated protocol. Log against 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a validity paragraph written from memory that does not match the protocol, found on audit.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can teach a consistent annotation shorthand and explain why contemporaneous records carry weight that reconstructed ones do not.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you realise you did not record something: add a dated, clearly marked note saying it was written afterwards, and weaken any claim that depends on it.",
"error": "Writing up adaptations from memory after the session and presenting them as the record.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'State the effect on validity in the report', and into Ethical Practice — record-keeping.",
"evidence":"Annotated protocol · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Adapt standardised procedure and record the adaptation","Re-evaluate whether to continue at all"): {
"why":   "Sometimes the right adaptation is to stop. A child who is distressed, unwell, withdrawing assent, or unable to access the task at all is not giving you valid data, and continuing may harm them and their trust in adults who assess them. Assent is ongoing, not a form signed at the start. Trainees often find stopping hardest because it feels like failing the assessment. Stopping well is a professional decision, not a failure.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — whether your supervisor stops or reschedules a session, and how they explain it to the child, the parent and the school.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the signs that led to stopping, what was said to the child, and what was planned instead.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what's the threshold where you stop rather than adapt?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — watching for signs that assent has been withdrawn, and saying so to your supervisor, quietly or at a break.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the decision to stop, and the conversation with the family and school.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — noticing withdrawn assent is something you can do from the first session. Stopping has consequences — rescheduling, waiting lists, school expectations — that your supervisor manages.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — make the call to continue, adapt or stop in your own session, with your supervisor present to support.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — continuing because the school is waiting for the report, or because stopping feels like admitting something went wrong.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'do you want to check with them whether they'd like to keep going?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — keep checking assent through the session, stop when the child cannot or will not continue validly, and record the reason.\n\nPASS TEST — You were willing to stop\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the protocol note and a case note of the reason and the plan. Log against 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice. Consider Appendix 8 if it raised a dilemma.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a distressed child pushed through the battery, and scores reported as though the session were typical.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain ongoing assent, the signs that it has been withdrawn, and why stopping protects both the child and the validity of the assessment.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when a school or parent pushes back on a stopped session: explain that invalid scores would harm the child more than a delay, and offer a plan for completing the assessment.",
"error": "Continuing a session the child has clearly withdrawn from because the report is due.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into Ethical Practice — consent, assent and the child's welfare — and into 'State the effect on validity in the report'.",
"evidence":"Protocol note · case note · Appendix 8 if a dilemma arose · Log sheet tagged 1. Assessment and 6. Ethical Practice"},

# ── 6. ETHICAL PRACTICE · Garda vetting and Children First certification ──

("Garda vetting and Children First certification","Submit vetting early enough to clear before day one"): {
"why":   "Under the National Vetting Bureau (Children and Vulnerable Persons) Acts 2012–2016, a relevant organisation must not let a person do relevant work with children until it has received a vetting disclosure for that person. That is a legal bar, not a formality. You apply through the organisation's liaison person, usually by e-vetting, and processing time varies with demand, so no fixed figure is given here. Check with the placement. Arrive with vetting pending and you cannot see children, and the lost days come off your placement.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how the placement handles vetting for you: who their liaison person is, whether they accept a disclosure from UCD or need their own, and what proof they want on day one.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the liaison person's name, the date you submitted, the date of the disclosure, and which organisation it was issued to.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'does a vetting disclosure from another organisation cover me here, or do I need yours?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — completing the forms, supplying ID and addresses accurately, and chasing progress.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the organisation's decision that you are cleared to start relevant work.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — the application is yours and mistakes in it cause most of the delay. The legal decision to let you work with children belongs to the organisation.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — submit vetting for the next placement without being reminded, as soon as the placement is confirmed.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — leaving it until the month before, and assuming last year's disclosure transfers.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — the placement co-ordinator asking, 'where are you with vetting for Cavan?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — for every placement, confirm what vetting that organisation needs, apply early, and hold the disclosure before your first day.\n\nPASS TEST — Cleared, not pending\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the disclosure (or the organisation's confirmation) on your placement file. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — Cavan starts on 05/10/2026 and you spend the first week unable to see a child.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain to a new trainee what the Vetting Acts require, why it is the organisation's disclosure that counts, and how to plan the timing.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when vetting has not cleared by day one: tell the placement and the university at once, and agree non-contact work (induction, file reading, policy) so no one is put in a position where you see children uncleared.",
"error": "Starting a placement on the assumption that vetting from an earlier placement carries over.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Complete service induction' and into every direct-contact skill in the file.",
"evidence":"Vetting disclosure on placement file · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Garda vetting and Children First certification","Complete Children First training and file the certificate"): {
"why":   "The Children First Act 2015 and the Children First National Guidance (DCYA, 2017) set out how anyone working with children recognises and reports concerns. Tusla provides a free e-learning module, 'Introduction to Children First', which issues a certificate. Placements and universities expect it before you start. More importantly, it is the minimum grounding for the child protection skills below. Check with UCD whether it needs refreshing each year or other training on top.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — which child protection training your supervisor and the service require, and whether the service adds its own policy training.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the course, provider, date completed, and any renewal requirement.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'is there anything in this service's child safeguarding statement that the Tusla module doesn't cover?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — completing the module properly (not clicking through) and filing the certificate.\n\nTHEY HOLD — confirming that your training meets the service's requirement.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — the training is yours. Whether it is enough for this service is their call, because services often have extra requirements.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — complete and file the training before each placement, and read the service's Child Safeguarding Statement in the first week.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — a certificate that exists but whose content you cannot recall when a concern comes up.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'what would you do if a child told you something in a session this afternoon?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — keep a current certificate, dated before the placement starts, on file, and know where the service's safeguarding statement is.\n\nPASS TEST — Certificate dated before placement\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the certificate on the placement file and in your portfolio. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a certificate dated after your first day of child contact, found at placement audit.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain what the Children First training covers, how it relates to the Act and the Guidance, and what each service adds through its own safeguarding statement.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you find your training is out of date mid-placement: tell your supervisor, complete it at once, and take their direction on contact in the meantime.",
"error": "Completing the module after child contact has already begun.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Know the Tusla reporting thresholds' and 'Recognise and act on child protection concerns'.",
"evidence":"Children First certificate on placement file · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Garda vetting and Children First certification","Know the Tusla reporting thresholds"): {
"why":   "The Children First Act 2015 defines harm as assault, ill-treatment, neglect or sexual abuse. For mandated persons it sets thresholds: for most categories the child's health, development or welfare must have been, or be at risk of being, seriously affected; for sexual abuse there is no 'serious' qualifier. Separately, the Guidance asks anyone with reasonable grounds for concern to report to Tusla. Read the thresholds in Children First National Guidance (2017) chapter 3 in full — they include specific provisions, such as for 16–17-year-olds, that are not summarised here.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor talks through a concern: whether it is 'reasonable grounds for concern' (report) or at the mandated threshold (report as a mandated person), and how they use the Guidance.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the four categories of harm and the threshold wording for each, from the Guidance, not from memory.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what's the hardest threshold call you've made, and what tipped it?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — reading the Guidance thresholds and testing yourself on anonymised case examples, then checking your answers with your supervisor.\n\nTHEY HOLD — live threshold judgements until you have shown you can make them. The legal duty to report is still yours if you are a mandated person.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — threshold knowledge can be rehearsed safely. Real calls need practised judgement, but the duty itself is never delegated.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — for a live concern, state your threshold judgement and your reasoning to your supervisor the same day.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — waiting for certainty before reporting. The test is reasonable grounds, not proof.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'you've described it as not serious enough. Which threshold wording are you using?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — state the category of harm and whether it meets the threshold, citing the Guidance wording, and act on it.\n\nPASS TEST — You can state what reaches the threshold\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — supervision record of threshold discussions. Log against 6. Ethical Practice. Do not record identifiable case details in your personal log.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a concern at threshold not reported because you were waiting to be sure.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain the four categories, the mandated thresholds, the 'reasonable grounds' standard for everyone, and the specific provisions — citing the Guidance chapter, not paraphrasing from memory.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you are unsure: consult the DLP or Tusla (Tusla will discuss a concern informally), and when in doubt, report.",
"error": "Waiting for proof when the test is reasonable grounds for concern.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Know you are a mandated person and what that means' and 'Recognise a concern against the threshold'.",
"evidence":"Supervision record · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Garda vetting and Children First certification","Know you are a mandated person and what that means"): {
"why":   "Schedule 2 of the Children First Act 2015 lists the classes of mandated persons, including psychologists. A mandated person who knows, believes or has reasonable grounds to suspect harm at threshold must report it to Tusla as soon as practicable. Telling the DLP does not discharge that duty, though a joint report with the DLP is allowed. Whether a trainee on placement is a mandated person in their own right depends on the Schedule 2 wording. Confirm it in writing with UCD and the placement, and act as if you are until told otherwise.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor describes their own duty, and how the service handles joint reports with the DLP.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the exact Schedule 2 wording that covers psychologists, and UCD's written position on whether it covers trainees.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'if you and the DLP disagreed about whether to report, what would you do?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — getting UCD's written position on your status, and reading section 14 of the Act (the duty to report) yourself.\n\nTHEY HOLD — nothing in the duty itself. If you are a mandated person, the duty is yours. What your supervisor holds is support and advice.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — this is the one place in the file where the usual split does not apply. The law makes the duty personal.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — when a concern arises, you decide whether your duty is engaged and make or join the report, with your supervisor supporting.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — handing it to the DLP and assuming that is the end of your part.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'have you confirmed the report went in, and is your name on it?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — make, or confirm you are named on, a report to Tusla for any concern at threshold, as soon as practicable, and keep your record.\n\nPASS TEST — The duty is personal, not delegable\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — UCD's written confirmation of your status on file; your own record of any report. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — the DLP decides not to report, you do nothing more, and the concern was at threshold.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain Schedule 2, section 14, joint reporting, and the difference between the DLP's role and the mandated person's own duty.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when the DLP will not report and you believe the threshold is met: report to Tusla yourself, tell your supervisor, and record it.",
"error": "Believing that telling the DLP discharges your own duty as a mandated person.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Act the same day without waiting for supervision' and 'Report to the DLP and record the time'.",
"evidence":"UCD written confirmation of status · own report record · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

# ── 6. ETHICAL PRACTICE · Complete service induction ──

("Complete service induction","Complete health and safety and lone working induction"): {
"why":   "Educational psychologists work alone in schools, homes and cars. The service's lone working procedure exists so someone knows where you are, when you are due back, and what to do if you are not. CORU SoP 3.13 and 3.14 place safe practice on you as well as the employer. Home visits carry particular risk: an unknown environment, other adults present, pets, and no colleague nearby. Skipping induction means you will not know the procedure on the day you need it.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — what your supervisor does before a home visit: who they tell, the check-in time, what they take, where they park, and the code word or exit plan if the service has one.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the service's lone working procedure step by step: sign-out, check-in, escalation if you miss a check-in.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'have you ever left a visit early? What made you go?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — following the sign-out and check-in procedure for joint visits yourself.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the risk assessment for the visit and the decision on whether it goes ahead.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — following the procedure builds the habit while someone is with you. Assessing risk from a referral needs knowledge of the area and the family that you do not yet have.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — do the pre-visit risk check yourself and present it to your supervisor before a visit you lead.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — skipping sign-out for a 'quick' visit, and not reading the file for risk information.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'is there anything on file about other adults in the house?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — follow the lone working procedure for every visit, and read the file for risk information before you go.\n\nPASS TEST — You know the procedure for a home visit\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — induction checklist signed. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — you are at a home visit that becomes unsafe and nobody knows where you are.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can walk a new trainee through the procedure, explain why each step exists, and describe the warning signs that should end a visit early.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when a visit feels unsafe: leave, without explaining in detail if need be, contact your check-in person, and record and report it.",
"error": "Doing a 'quick' home visit without signing out because it was only twenty minutes.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Identify the DLP by name in week one' and into Working with Others — home-based work.",
"evidence":"Signed induction checklist · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Complete service induction","Identify the DLP by name in week one"): {
"why":   "Children First National Guidance (2017) says organisations providing services to children should appoint a Designated Liaison Person (DLP) for child protection. Schools must appoint a DLP and a deputy DLP under the Department of Education's Child Protection Procedures for Primary and Post-Primary Schools 2017 (revised 2023); the DLP is normally the principal. Your placement service will also have its own DLP. When a concern arises you have minutes, not days, to act. Having to find out who the DLP is at that moment costs time and adds stress. The UCD Handbook §6.2 expects you to know this — read that section for the exact requirement.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — who your supervisor names as DLP for the service, and how they find the DLP for each school they visit.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the service DLP and deputy, with phone numbers, and the DLP for every school on your caseload.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'if a school's DLP isn't available, who do you go to?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — building the DLP list for your schools, confirming each name with the school office.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the service-level contact and any escalation.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — building the list is a simple task that should be yours from day one. The escalation routes are the service's to set.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — keep the DLP list current and check the DLP when you first visit each new school.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — a list made once and never updated when schools change principal.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'who's the DLP at the school you're going to this morning?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — have the DLP and deputy for the service and every school written down, where you can reach them without a laptop, by the end of week one.\n\nPASS TEST — Written down, not looked up later\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the DLP list, kept securely. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a child discloses and you spend twenty minutes finding out who to tell.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain the DLP role, how it differs from your own duty as a mandated person, and why the list must exist before it is needed.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when neither DLP can be reached: your own duty to report to Tusla still stands, so report directly and tell the DLP afterwards.",
"error": "Not knowing the school's DLP when you are on site with a concern.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Report to the DLP and record the time' and into 'Act the same day without waiting for supervision'.",
"evidence":"DLP list · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Complete service induction","Learn the referral and risk pathways"): {
"why":   "You will meet needs you cannot meet: self-harm, suicidal thinking, mental illness, disability, family crisis. Each has a route, such as CAMHS, primary care psychology, the Children's Disability Network Team, Tusla, the GP or emergency services, and each route has criteria, a way in and a waiting time. Knowing that a pathway exists is not enough. You need to know what happens after you refer, or you cannot tell the family what to expect or judge whether the referral is the right one.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor decides where to refer, and what they tell the family about what happens next.\n\nWRITE DOWN — for each main pathway: who can refer, how, the criteria, what happens next, the likely wait, and what to do in an emergency.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'which referral route do trainees most often misunderstand here?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — building a one-page pathway map for the service and checking it with your supervisor.\n\nTHEY HOLD — referral decisions for your cases, and any urgent risk response.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — mapping is research you can do alone. Referral decisions and risk responses carry consequences you need to have seen handled first.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — propose the referral route for your case and explain to your supervisor what will happen after it.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — referring to a service whose criteria the child does not meet, and not knowing the emergency route for suicide risk.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'what does CAMHS need to see in the referral to accept it?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — refer by the right route, and tell the family accurately what will happen next and how long it may take.\n\nPASS TEST — You know what happens after you refer\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the pathway map and referral records. Log against 6. Ethical Practice and 5. Working with Others.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a referral rejected weeks later for not meeting criteria, and a family left waiting for nothing.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain the local pathways, their criteria and what to expect, and the emergency route for risk.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when a referral is rejected: find out why, tell the family, and find another route rather than closing the case.",
"error": "Referring without knowing the service's criteria, so the referral bounces and the family waits for nothing.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Refer on and maintain responsibility until contact is made' and into Working with Others.",
"evidence":"Pathway map · referral records · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice and 5. Working with Others"},

("Complete service induction","Learn the records, IT and information governance policy"): {
"why":   "Psychological records are special category personal data under the GDPR (Article 9) and the Data Protection Act 2018. You need to know where records are kept, who can see them, how long they are kept, how they are destroyed, and what to do if data is lost. Retention periods are set by the service's policy, and HSE and Department of Education services differ, so check rather than assume. A lost notebook or an email to the wrong address is a reportable data breach.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — where your supervisor keeps notes, protocols and reports; what they carry out of the office; how they send reports; and what they never email.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the service's rules on storage, transport, email, retention and breach reporting, and the Data Protection Officer's contact.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what's the most common data mistake you see from new staff?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — following the policy for your own notes and protocols from the first day.\n\nTHEY HOLD — decisions on sharing information with other agencies.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — handling your own records is a daily habit. Sharing across agencies has legal conditions that need someone who knows them.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — manage your caseload records to policy; your supervisor spot-checks.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — protocols in the car overnight, notes on a personal device, identifiable details in your own learning log.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'where are the protocols from this morning right now?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — store, transport, send and retain records to policy, and know what to do if something is lost.\n\nPASS TEST — You know where records live and for how long\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — induction checklist signed. Log against 6. Ethical Practice. Keep identifiers out of your own log.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a report emailed to the wrong parent, and you do not know who to tell.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain why psychological records are special category data, the service's retention and transport rules, and the breach procedure.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when data is lost or misdirected: tell your supervisor and the Data Protection Officer at once, because the controller has 72 hours to notify the Data Protection Commission where required (GDPR Art. 33).",
"error": "Keeping identifiable case details in your own learning log or on a personal device.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into record-keeping throughout — 'Record what was explained', 'Record verbatim what was said or seen, at the time'.",
"evidence":"Signed induction checklist · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Complete service induction","Ask which tools you may administer independently here"): {
"why":   "Test publishers restrict who may buy and use their instruments, and services add their own rules about what trainees may do unsupervised. PSI Code 3.3.6 is the standard mapped here — not to be confused with PSI accreditation criterion 3.3.6 on placement settings; read the Code clause itself before citing it. If this is not agreed in writing, a trainee can end up giving a test they are not permitted to use alone, and the scores may not be usable. The Placement Plan is where this belongs, agreed at the start.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — which tools your supervisor uses, which they would let a trainee administer, and which they would not.\n\nWRITE DOWN — every tool you might use, with the publisher's qualification level and the service's rule for trainees.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'is there a tool here that you'd never let a trainee give alone, and why?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — drafting the list of tools and your proposed level of independence for each.\n\nTHEY HOLD — agreeing the list and signing the Placement Plan.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — drafting shows your knowledge of the tools. The decision rests on their responsibility for your work.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — raise the tools list in the first placement planning meeting and get it agreed.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — assuming that because you gave a test at the last placement you can give it alone here.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'is that in the Placement Plan yet?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — have the list of tools, and the level of independence for each, written into the Placement Plan in the first two weeks.\n\nPASS TEST — Agreed in writing in the Placement Plan\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the signed Placement Plan. Log against 6. Ethical Practice and 1. Assessment.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — you administer a test alone and your supervisor says afterwards that you were not cleared for it.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain publisher qualification levels, how services add their own rules, and why agreeing this in writing protects the child, the trainee and the supervisor.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you are asked to give a tool not on the agreed list: say so, and ask for it to be added or supervised.",
"error": "Assuming last placement's tool clearances carry over to this one.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Know your publisher competence level for every tool' and into Assessment administration skills.",
"evidence":"Signed Placement Plan · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice and 1. Assessment"},

# ── 6. ETHICAL PRACTICE · Obtain informed consent and assent ──

("Obtain informed consent and assent","Explain purpose, process and who will see the report"): {
"why":   "Consent is only informed if the person knows what they are agreeing to: why you are involved, what you will do, how long it will take, what will be written, and who will read it. PSI Code 1.3.5 is the clause mapped here. Parents often assume the report stays with them, or that the school sees everything, or nothing. Someone who finds out afterwards who read the report has not given informed consent, and trust is hard to rebuild.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor explains involvement at the first meeting: the words they use, the order, and what they check the parent understood.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the explanation as near verbatim as you can, and especially how they describe who receives the report.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what do parents most often misunderstand about who sees the report?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — the explanation of process and timing, from a script you have checked with your supervisor.\n\nTHEY HOLD — questions about sharing with other agencies, and any hesitation from the parent.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — the process explanation can be scripted. Questions about information-sharing need someone who knows the service policy exactly.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — give the whole explanation and take the consent, with your supervisor present.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — jargon, rushing, and consent taken before the explanation has finished.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'can I just check — did you follow who'll get a copy?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — explain purpose, process and who will see the report in plain words, check understanding, and only then take consent.\n\nPASS TEST — In plain words, before anything begins\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the consent form and a note of what was explained. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a parent upset at the feedback meeting because they did not know the school would get the report.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain the elements of informed consent, why each matters, and how to check understanding without making the parent feel tested.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when a parent realises later they did not understand: stop, re-explain, and let them re-consent or withdraw.",
"error": "Taking a signature before explaining who will see the report.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'State the right to stop or withdraw', 'Record what was explained, not just that consent was given', and Working with Others.",
"evidence":"Consent form · note of explanation · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Obtain informed consent and assent","State the right to stop or withdraw"): {
"why":   "Consent is ongoing. A parent, young person or child can stop a session or withdraw from involvement at any point, and must be told so at the start (PSI Code 1.3.5). Families often feel they cannot say no to a psychologist the school has brought in, especially where they fear it will affect support. Unless the right to withdraw is stated, consent is not truly voluntary. Families who know they can stop are, in practice, more likely to stay.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — whether and how your supervisor tells families and children they can stop, and whether they repeat it later.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the words they use for adults and the words they use for children.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'has anyone ever withdrawn? How did you handle what the school expected?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — saying the right-to-withdraw sentence to the parent and the child, from a script checked by your supervisor.\n\nTHEY HOLD — what happens if someone does withdraw, including the conversation with the school.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — the statement is short and can be practised word for word. Handling a withdrawal involves the school relationship and service consequences.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — state the right to stop, to parent and child, at the start and again before any demanding task.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — mentioning it so quickly, or so formally, that nobody could act on it.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — your supervisor adding, to the child, 'and you can say stop to us too.'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — tell every parent and child, in words they understand, that they can stop or withdraw at any time without it affecting the help they get, and record that you said it.\n\nPASS TEST — It was mentioned\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the note of consent discussion. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a parent who wanted to stop but did not know they could, and complains afterwards.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain why the right to withdraw is part of valid consent, how to say it so it is heard, and how to adapt it for a young child.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when someone withdraws: accept it without pressure, record it, tell the referrer only what the family agrees to, and leave the door open.",
"error": "Stating the right to withdraw so briefly that no one could act on it.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Recognise and respond to non-verbal withdrawal' and 'Re-evaluate whether to continue at all'.",
"evidence":"Note of consent discussion · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Obtain informed consent and assent","State the limits of confidentiality before disclosure"): {
"why":   "Confidentiality has limits: risk of serious harm to the person or others, child protection concerns, and legal requirements such as a court order. Those limits must be explained before someone discloses, not after (PSI Code 1.2.9; CORU SoP 1.12). A young person who tells you something and then learns you have to pass it on may feel betrayed, and may not tell another adult again. Explaining the limits first lets them choose what to say, knowing what will happen.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor explains the limits to a parent, to a teenager and to a young child. Notice how the words change with age.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the limits they list, and the child-friendly version.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'how do you explain limits without making a child think you expect them to disclose something?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — explaining the limits to a young person from a script checked with your supervisor.\n\nTHEY HOLD — responding to anything disclosed.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — the explanation can be practised. Responding to a disclosure needs the child protection skills below, which you build next.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — explain the limits yourself at the start of every session with a child or young person, with your supervisor present.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — leaving it out with young children, and 'everything's confidential' said as reassurance.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — your supervisor adding the limits if you forgot.",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — explain the limits at the start, in age-appropriate words, before any conversation that could lead to disclosure.\n\nPASS TEST — First, not after\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the note of the explanation. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a teenager tells you about self-harm after you said everything was confidential, and now you have to break that.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain the limits, the reason for stating them first, and how to phrase them for different ages.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when a disclosure comes before you explained the limits: do not promise secrecy, explain honestly now what you must do, and tell them what happens next.",
"error": "Promising a child that 'everything you say stays in this room'.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Do not investigate or question further' and 'Recognise a concern against the threshold'.",
"evidence":"Note of consent and confidentiality discussion · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Obtain informed consent and assent","Establish who holds guardianship before contact"): {
"why":   "The person bringing the child is not always the person who can consent. Under the Guardianship of Infants Act 1964, as amended by the Children and Family Relationships Act 2015, a mother is automatically a guardian, as is a father married to her. An unmarried father may or may not be: he becomes a guardian automatically only if he has lived with the mother for 12 consecutive months after 18/01/2016, including at least 3 months with the mother and child after the birth; otherwise only by statutory declaration with the mother or by court order. For a child in care, Tusla may hold consent rights depending on the type of order. The exact rules have conditions not set out here, so check with the service rather than relying on this summary.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor checks guardianship at referral: what they ask, what they record, and what they do when parents are separated.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the questions they ask and where the answer is recorded.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what do you do when one guardian consents and the other objects?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — checking the referral form and file for guardianship, and flagging anything unclear.\n\nTHEY HOLD — decisions where guardianship is disputed or where a child is in care.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — checking the file is routine. Disputed guardianship and care orders need legal knowledge and service policy.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — confirm guardianship yourself before first contact, with your supervisor checking.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — assuming the parent who made contact is the only guardian, and not asking about a child in care.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'the form says parents separated. Have you checked with dad?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — confirm and record who holds guardianship, and whose consent is needed, before any contact with the child.\n\nPASS TEST — You checked rather than assumed\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the guardianship note on the case file. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a complaint from a guardian who did not know their child had been assessed.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain the basic rules on guardianship, the common complications (separation, unmarried fathers, care orders) and where to get authoritative advice.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you discover mid-involvement that a guardian was not consulted: stop, tell your supervisor, and contact the guardian before continuing.",
"error": "Assuming the parent who phoned the school is the only person whose consent is needed.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Explain purpose, process and who will see the report' and 'Record what was explained'.",
"evidence":"Guardianship note on case file · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Obtain informed consent and assent","Use supported decision-making where capacity is in question"): {
"why":   "The Assisted Decision-Making (Capacity) Act 2015, commenced on 26/04/2023, applies to adults. It is relevant to young adults on your caseload (UCD Table 3). It presumes capacity, requires all practicable steps to help a person decide before concluding they cannot, and says an unwise decision is not proof of incapacity (section 8). Capacity is judged decision by decision: can the person understand, retain, use and weigh the information, and communicate a decision (section 3)? PSI Code 1.3.10 is the clause mapped here. Assuming incapacity takes away a right.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — what your supervisor does to help a young adult with an intellectual disability or communication need to understand and decide: easy-read materials, time, a trusted person present, visual supports.\n\nWRITE DOWN — each support used and how the young person showed their decision.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'how do you tell the difference between someone who can't decide and someone who needs more help to?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — preparing accessible materials (easy-read, visuals) for the consent conversation.\n\nTHEY HOLD — any judgement about capacity, and any involvement of decision supporters.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — preparing materials is concrete and checkable. A capacity judgement has legal weight.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — lead the consent conversation using supports, with your supervisor present.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — speaking to the carer instead of the young person, and taking the carer's view as consent.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — your supervisor redirecting: 'let's ask Seán what he thinks.'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — presume capacity, use every practicable support, and record how you supported the decision.\n\nPASS TEST — How, not just that\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the note recording supports used and how the decision was expressed. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — 'consent obtained from mother' for a nineteen-year-old who was never asked.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain the ADMCA 2015 principles, the functional test, and the practical supports that help someone decide — citing sections and the Decision Support Service guidance.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when, even with support, the young person cannot decide: record the steps taken, involve your supervisor, and follow the Act's framework rather than defaulting to a carer's say-so.",
"error": "Taking a carer's consent for an adult who was never supported to decide for themselves.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Obtain assent from the child in their own language' and into Working with Others — young adults and families.",
"evidence":"Note of supports used · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Obtain informed consent and assent","Recognise and respond to non-verbal withdrawal"): {
"why":   "Many children will not say 'stop'. They withdraw assent by turning away, going quiet, putting their head down, fidgeting, looking at the door, or saying 'I don't know' to everything. Younger children and children with communication needs may have no other way to tell you. PSI Code 1.4.3 is the clause mapped here. Carrying on past these signs overrides the child's wishes and produces data that is not valid.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — the non-verbal signs your supervisor responds to, and what they do: pause, check in, offer a choice, stop.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the sign, what your supervisor did, and how the child responded.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'which signs do you treat as a no, and which just as needing a break?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — watching for signs during your supervisor's session and telling them what you saw afterwards.\n\nTHEY HOLD — responding in the session.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — you learn the signs fastest when you are not also administering. Responding in the moment is the next step.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — watch for signs in your own session and respond — pause, name what you notice, offer a choice — with your supervisor present.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — reading withdrawal as 'being difficult' or 'avoidance of hard items' and pressing on, because the subtest is nearly finished.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'I think she might want a rest. Shall we check?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — respond to non-verbal withdrawal by checking in and, if it continues, stopping — and record what you saw.\n\nPASS TEST — You stopped\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — protocol annotation and case note. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a completed protocol from a child who had clearly withdrawn, with no note that they had.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can describe the common non-verbal signs of withdrawal, how they vary with age and need, and how to check in without pressure.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you realise afterwards that you missed withdrawal: note it, reconsider the validity of the data, and tell your supervisor.",
"error": "Treating a child's silence and turning away as defiance to work through.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Re-evaluate whether to continue at all' and 'Recognise when standard procedure is not working'.",
"evidence":"Protocol annotation · case note · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Obtain informed consent and assent","Obtain assent from the child in their own language"): {
"why":   "Children have the right to express views in matters affecting them (UNCRC Article 12). Assent is their agreement, given in terms they understand, alongside the consent of guardians. 'Their own language' means literally their home language, or Irish, or sign language or AAC, and also words pitched at their age and understanding. Assent sought in words the child did not understand is not assent. A child who was never asked has been done to, not worked with.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor asks a child for assent: the words, visual supports, how they check understanding, and what they do if the child hesitates.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the phrasing for different ages, and any supports for language or communication.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'have you worked with an interpreter for a child? What did you need to do differently?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — preparing child-friendly explanations and visual supports for assent, checked with your supervisor.\n\nTHEY HOLD — arranging interpreters, and decisions where a child says no but guardians have consented.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — preparing materials is concrete. A conflict between the child's no and the parents' yes needs judgement and relationship.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — ask the child for assent yourself, with your supervisor present.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — asking in adult language, and not asking at all because the parent has consented.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'can you tell me back what we're going to do today?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — ask every child for assent in words and a language or mode they understand, check their understanding, and record their answer.\n\nPASS TEST — The child was asked\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — note of the child's assent in the case record. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — the child tells a teacher later that they did not know what the psychologist was doing with them.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain assent versus consent, Article 12, and how to adapt for age, language, and communication needs.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when a child refuses despite parental consent: respect it for now, explore why, and plan with the family, rather than overriding it.",
"error": "Taking the parent's consent as enough and not asking the child.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Record what was explained, not just that consent was given' and into 'Include the child's own self-report where age-appropriate'.",
"evidence":"Note of child's assent · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Obtain informed consent and assent","Record what was explained, not just that consent was given"): {
"why":   "A tick box saying 'consent obtained' proves nothing about whether consent was informed. If a complaint arises, the question is what the person was told. CORU SoP 1.15 is the standard mapped here. A good consent note records what was explained (purpose, process, who sees the report, limits of confidentiality, right to withdraw), who was present, questions asked, and the answer given. That note protects the family, the child and you.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — what your supervisor writes in the file after a consent conversation, and how long after the conversation they write it.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the headings they use (explained, present, questions, answer), and anything they add beyond the signed form.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'have you ever needed a consent note to answer a complaint? What did it need to show?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — writing the consent note after a conversation your supervisor led.\n\nTHEY HOLD — checking the note, and adding anything you missed.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — writing the note after watching tests whether you noticed what was said. Their check shows you what you missed.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — take consent and write the note yourself; your supervisor reviews it.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — a note that records the outcome (consent given) and not the content.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'if the mother complained next year, would this note show what she was told?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — after every consent conversation, write a note that records the content: what was explained, who was present, questions, and the answer.\n\nPASS TEST — The note shows the content\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the consent note in the case record. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a file that says 'consent given' and nothing else, when the parent says they were never told the school would see the report.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain why content matters more than the tick, and show a peer a short template for a good consent note.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you find a thin consent note on your own file: add a dated addendum with what you remember, clearly marked as written later.",
"error": "Recording 'consent obtained' with no record of what was explained.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into record-keeping throughout, and into 'Keep notes at each stage' in ethical decision-making.",
"evidence":"Consent note in case record · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

# ── 6. ETHICAL PRACTICE · Recognise and act on child protection concerns ──

("Recognise and act on child protection concerns","Recognise a concern against the threshold"): {
"why":   "Concerns rarely arrive as clear disclosures. They come as an injury with a vague explanation, a child who flinches, hunger, a comment in a drawing, a change in behaviour, or something a parent says. Children First National Guidance (2017) lists signs for each category of harm. Read that list, not a summary. The skill is noticing, then holding what you noticed against the threshold for 'reasonable grounds for concern'. Missing it leaves a child unprotected; noticing and not acting is worse.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — what your supervisor notices in a session or a file that makes them pause, and what they do next.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the observation, and which category of harm and which threshold they considered (without identifying details in your own log).\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what's the smallest thing that has ever led you to a report?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — noticing and naming anything that concerns you, to your supervisor, the same day.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the threshold judgement, while you are learning it. Your own duty to report stands regardless.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — noticing is something you can do from day one. Threshold judgement needs practice, but never at the cost of not acting.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — make the threshold judgement yourself and state it to your supervisor with your reasons.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — explaining signs away ('he's always clumsy') and waiting for more before saying anything.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'if you're wondering, that's the concern. What category is it?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — notice, name the category of harm, judge it against the threshold, and act — the same day.\n\nPASS TEST — You knew it when you saw it\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — your contemporaneous record and the report record. Log against 6. Ethical Practice. No identifiers in your own log.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — you noted something in a session, thought about it for a week, and then it was too late to report as soon as practicable.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain the categories, signs and thresholds from the Guidance, and why 'reasonable grounds' is a lower bar than certainty.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you realise later that something you saw was a concern: act now, record why it was delayed, and tell your supervisor.",
"error": "Explaining away a sign because the family seemed nice.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Do not investigate or question further', 'Record verbatim what was said or seen, at the time', and 'Act the same day'.",
"evidence":"Contemporaneous record · report record · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Recognise and act on child protection concerns","Do not investigate or question further"): {
"why":   "When a child discloses, your role is to listen, reassure, and pass it on — not to investigate. Investigation belongs to Tusla and An Garda Síochána. Leading or repeated questions can contaminate the child's account and harm a later investigation or prosecution. They can also distress the child. Children First National Guidance (2017) sets out how to respond: listen, do not ask leading questions, do not promise confidentiality, tell the child what happens next.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor would respond to a disclosure. You are unlikely to see one, so role-play it or talk it through.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the words your supervisor would use: acknowledging, not leading, not promising, and explaining what happens next.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what's the question you most want to ask in that moment, and why shouldn't you?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — rehearsing a disclosure response in role play with your supervisor playing the child, including a child who stops mid-sentence.\n\nTHEY HOLD — feedback on every question you asked and every promise you made.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — this is a skill you must do alone the first time it happens, with no one to hand it to, so rehearsal is the main way to learn it.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — role-play your response to different disclosures (partial, retracted, about a sibling), with your supervisor coaching.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — 'did he hurt you?', 'where did he touch you?', 'are you sure?' — any question that leads or tests.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'stop there. What did that question just put in her head?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — if a child discloses: listen, use open prompts only if needed ('tell me more'), do not promise secrecy, tell them what happens next, and do not interview.\n\nPASS TEST — You did not interview the child\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — your contemporaneous record. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a record full of your questions, so the child's account can no longer be separated from your prompting.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain why leading questions contaminate a child's account and how to respond using open prompts only.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you realise you asked a leading question: record exactly what you asked and what the child said, and tell the DLP and Tusla so they know.",
"error": "Asking 'did he hurt you?' in an effort to be sure before reporting.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Record verbatim what was said or seen, at the time' and 'Report to the DLP and record the time'.",
"evidence":"Contemporaneous record · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Recognise and act on child protection concerns","Record verbatim what was said or seen, at the time"): {
"why":   "Your record may be read by Tusla, An Garda Síochána or a court. A record made at the time, in the child's own words, with what you saw described factually, carries weight. A paraphrase written days later does not. CORU SoP 2.8 and 3.12 are the standards mapped here. The record must separate fact (what was said, what was seen) from your opinion, and be dated, timed and signed.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — what a good child protection record looks like. Ask your supervisor to show you an anonymised example or the service template.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the headings: date, time, place, who was present, the child's exact words, what you saw, what you said, who you told and when.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what makes a record useless to Tusla?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — writing practice records from role-played disclosures, and having your supervisor critique them.\n\nTHEY HOLD — feedback on factual accuracy and separation of fact and opinion.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — writing the record is a skill that can be practised to standard before you need it.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — write the record after a role-played or real concern, straight away, and have it checked.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — interpretation in place of description ('she seemed scared of her dad'), and paraphrase in place of the child's words.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'is that what she said, or what you think she meant?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — record the child's exact words, what you saw, and what you said, as soon as possible — dated, timed and signed, with fact and opinion separate.\n\nPASS TEST — Contemporaneous and factual\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the record on the case file, per service policy. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a record written two days later: 'child indicated possible abuse at home'.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain what makes a child protection record usable, and why exact words and the separation of fact from opinion matter.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you could not record at the time: record as soon as possible, and note when the record was written and why it was delayed.",
"error": "Paraphrasing the child's words into your own summary.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Report to the DLP and record the time' and into record-keeping throughout.",
"evidence":"Record on case file · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Recognise and act on child protection concerns","Act the same day without waiting for supervision"): {
"why":   "The Children First Act 2015 requires a mandated person to report to Tusla 'as soon as practicable'. Your next supervision may be days away. Waiting for it can leave a child at risk overnight, and delay is a common way to fall short of the duty. Supervision is for support and learning after you act, not permission before. If your supervisor is available the same day, talk to them — but the timing is set by the concern, not by the diary.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how quickly your supervisor acts on a concern, and whether anything waits until the end of the day or week.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the timeline: when the concern arose, when the DLP was told, when Tusla was contacted.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what would you do if this came up at 4pm on a Friday?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — telling your supervisor and the DLP straight away, and being part of the report.\n\nTHEY HOLD — support and advice. If you are a mandated person, the duty to report is still yours.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — acting straight away is the non-negotiable part, and it is yours from the start.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — lead the same-day response, contacting your supervisor if available but not waiting if they are not.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — 'I'll bring it to supervision on Thursday.'\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'don't wait for me. Ring the DLP now and we'll talk after.'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — act the same day: DLP, and a report to Tusla if at threshold, whether or not your supervisor is reachable.\n\nPASS TEST — The duty does not wait\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the record with times, and the report. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a concern held until supervision three days later.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain 'as soon as practicable', why supervision follows action rather than preceding it, and what to do out of hours (Tusla out-of-hours service, or An Garda Síochána if a child is in immediate danger).\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you realise you have delayed: act now, record the delay honestly, and tell your supervisor.",
"error": "Holding a concern until the next supervision session.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Report to the DLP and record the time' and 'Bring it to supervision afterwards, not instead'.",
"evidence":"Record with times · report · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Recognise and act on child protection concerns","Report to the DLP and record the time"): {
"why":   "The DLP makes sure the organisation responds consistently, and makes or joins reports to Tusla. Telling them is part of the procedure in every school and service (Children First National Guidance, 2017). If you are a mandated person, telling the DLP does not replace your own duty, but a joint report can meet both. The record of who you told, and when, is what shows the duty was met. Tusla accepts reports through its online portal or by the standard report form — check the current route.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor reports to a DLP: in person or by phone, what they say, and what they write down afterwards.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the elements: whom you told, when, what you said, what they said they would do, and whether a Tusla report was made and by whom.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'how do you follow up to know the report went in?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — making the call to the DLP, with your supervisor there, and writing the record.\n\nTHEY HOLD — support in the conversation, and escalation if the DLP does not act.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — the call and the record are the core actions and should be yours. Escalating over a DLP needs confidence and knowledge of the service.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — report to the DLP yourself and record it, with your supervisor informed the same day.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — no time on the record, and no check that a Tusla report was made.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'what time did you speak to her, and is she reporting today?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — report to the DLP, record who and when, and confirm a Tusla report has been made (jointly or by you).\n\nPASS TEST — The file shows who and when\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the record on the case file, and the report reference if you have one. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — the file says 'discussed with principal' with no date, time, or outcome.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain the DLP's role, joint reporting, and exactly what a report record must contain.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you cannot confirm the Tusla report was made: make it yourself, and record that you did.",
"error": "Telling the DLP and not recording when, or whether a report followed.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Bring it to supervision afterwards, not instead'.",
"evidence":"Record on case file · report reference · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Recognise and act on child protection concerns","Bring it to supervision afterwards, not instead"): {
"why":   "Child protection work is distressing and the decisions are rarely clean. Supervision afterwards is where you check your judgement, process what you heard, and learn for the next time. The UCD Handbook §6.2 is the reference mapped here — read it for the programme's exact expectation. The order is what matters. Supervision instead of action is a breach. Action with no supervision after leaves you carrying it alone and learning nothing from it.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor uses their own supervision after a child protection case.\n\nWRITE DOWN — what they bring: the decision, the reasoning, the emotional impact.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what do you still carry from cases like this, and what helps?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — bringing the case to supervision: what happened, what you did and when, how it affected you.\n\nTHEY HOLD — review of your decision-making, and support.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — naming the impact is yours. Reviewing the decision is how they help you calibrate.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — lead the supervision discussion: timeline, threshold, action, what you would do differently.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — a factual account with no mention of impact on you, or a discussion that re-opens whether to act when action has already been taken.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'and how was it for you?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — after any child protection action, put it on the next supervision agenda, with the record, and reflect on both judgement and impact.\n\nPASS TEST — Supervision follows the action\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the supervision record. Consider an Appendix 8 entry if a dilemma arose. Log against 6. Ethical Practice and 7. Supervision.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — no mention of the case in supervision, and a trainee who carries it alone.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain why supervision comes after action, and what good supervision of a child protection case covers.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when a case stays with you: raise it again in supervision, and use the employee assistance or occupational health service.",
"error": "Using supervision as the place to decide whether to act.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Seek supervision when stressed, not only when stuck' and into Supervision.",
"evidence":"Supervision record · Appendix 8 if a dilemma arose · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice and 7. Supervision"},

# ── 6. ETHICAL PRACTICE · Work an ethical dilemma through a decision procedure ──

("Work an ethical dilemma through a decision procedure","Identify the issue and all affected parties"): {
"why":   "The PSI Code of Professional Ethics sets out an ethical decision-making procedure in Appendix A. Its first step is to identify the issue and everyone affected by it; the sheet maps Appendix B here too — read both in the Code itself. Dilemmas are usually framed around the obvious parties: the child and the parent. Missing a party — a sibling, a teacher, the service, the wider public, or you — means their interests are never weighed. Most poor decisions are poor at this first step.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor frames a dilemma when they first raise it, and who they name.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the dilemma in one sentence, and every party affected, including those your supervisor adds.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'who's the party people usually forget in a dilemma like this?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — writing the issue and listing all affected parties for a dilemma your supervisor is working.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the decision.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — listing parties is where trainees learn to look wider, and it is safe to practise on a live case your supervisor holds.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — frame a dilemma in your own case and present the issue and all the parties in supervision before discussing what to do.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — stopping at the child and the parent, and leaving out siblings, the teacher, the service, other children in the class, and yourself.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'who else is affected by what you decide — including you?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — for any dilemma, write the issue in one sentence and list every affected party, including the less obvious ones.\n\nPASS TEST — Including ones you would otherwise miss\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — Appendix 8 dilemma entry. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a decision that protected the child and parent and harmed a sibling nobody had thought about.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can walk a peer through step 1 on a worked case, and show the prompts that surface hidden parties: who else lives there, who else is in the room, who pays, who is responsible if it goes wrong.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when a party emerges late: add them, and go back through the later steps rather than bolting them on.",
"error": "Framing a dilemma around the child and parent only.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Scan the Code for the clauses that apply' and 'Evaluate each party's rights and responsibilities'.",
"evidence":"Appendix 8 dilemma entry · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Work an ethical dilemma through a decision procedure","Scan the Code for the clauses that apply"): {
"why":   "Step 2 of the PSI Code Appendix A procedure is to check the Code for the clauses relevant to the issue. The Code is organised under four ethical principles — respect for the rights and dignity of the person, competence, responsibility, and integrity — each with numbered clauses. Citing clause numbers makes your reasoning checkable and shows it is grounded in the Code, not personal values. Dilemmas often turn on two clauses in tension, and you only see the tension once both are written down.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — whether your supervisor opens the Code itself when a dilemma arises, or works from memory, and how fast they find the relevant principle.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the clause numbers they cite, what each says, and where two clauses pull against each other.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'which clauses come up most often in EP work, and which are most often misread?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — searching the Code for relevant clauses for a live dilemma, and listing them with numbers.\n\nTHEY HOLD — interpretation of how the clauses apply.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — finding clauses builds your familiarity with the Code. Weighing them against each other is judgement.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — present the relevant clauses and the tension between them in supervision.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — citing one clause that supports what you wanted to do anyway.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'what clause would someone arguing the other side cite?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — cite every relevant Code clause by number, including those that pull against your preferred option.\n\nPASS TEST — You cite clause numbers\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — Appendix 8 dilemma entry with clauses. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — 'this would breach confidentiality' with no clause, and no mention of the clause that pulls the other way.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain the four principles, find clauses quickly, and show how two clauses can conflict.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when the Code does not answer the question: say so, and move to the rights and consequences analysis in the next steps.",
"error": "Citing only the clause that supports the option you had already chosen.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Evaluate each party's rights and responsibilities'.",
"evidence":"Appendix 8 dilemma entry · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Work an ethical dilemma through a decision procedure","Evaluate each party's rights and responsibilities"): {
"why":   "Step 3 of the Appendix A procedure is to consider the rights, responsibilities and welfare of each affected party. Taking each party separately stops the loudest voice setting the frame, and shows where interests truly conflict and where they only seem to. A child's right to privacy, a parent's right to information, a school's duty of care, and your own professional responsibility can all be real at once.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor weighs parties, and whether they give the child's interests explicit weight.\n\nWRITE DOWN — a table of parties, with each one's rights, responsibilities and interests.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'when a child's view and the parent's rights conflict, how do you weigh them?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — building the table for a live dilemma: one row per party, columns for rights, responsibilities, interests and welfare, with the source for each right (the Code, UNCRC, statute).\n\nTHEY HOLD — the weighting between rows.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — the table is analysis you can practise and have checked. Weighing competing rights needs experience of how such conflicts usually resolve.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — present the table and your view on which rights carry most weight, and why.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — treating adults' rights as automatically outweighing the child's.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'what does the child have a right to here?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — consider each party separately — rights, responsibilities, welfare — and write it down.\n\nPASS TEST — Each considered separately\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — Appendix 8 dilemma entry. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a decision where the child's view was never considered as a separate interest.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can walk a peer through a rights-and-responsibilities table, drawing on the UNCRC and the Code.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when rights cannot be reconciled: be explicit about whose rights are being limited and why, and record it.",
"error": "Treating the parent's view as the child's.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Generate as many alternative courses as possible'.",
"evidence":"Appendix 8 dilemma entry · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Work an ethical dilemma through a decision procedure","Generate as many alternative courses as possible"): {
"why":   "Step 4 of Appendix A is to generate alternatives. Under pressure, dilemmas collapse into two options: tell or do not tell, continue or stop. The best choice is often a third one nobody considered: tell with the young person's involvement, delay briefly to prepare, share part of the information, consult first. Generating alternatives is the step that most often turns a dilemma into a manageable decision.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how many options your supervisor considers, and whether they generate them before judging any. Judging too early is what shrinks the list to two.\n\nWRITE DOWN — every option they name, including the ones they reject quickly, and the unobvious ones: timing, partial sharing, involving the young person.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what's the option you didn't think of until later?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — brainstorming options for a live dilemma, on paper, before any are evaluated, aiming for at least five including some you would not choose.\n\nTHEY HOLD — adding options from their experience and choosing between them.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — generating options is creative and safe to practise. Choosing carries the consequence, so it stays with them for now.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — bring at least four options to supervision, written down, before discussing which is best.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — two options, one obviously right, so the 'dilemma' was never really one — or options that are the same thing reworded.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'what's a third way? What if you did it later, or with her, or only in part?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — list more than two options, including the unobvious ones, before evaluating any.\n\nPASS TEST — More than two\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — Appendix 8 dilemma entry. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a forced choice between two bad options when a better one existed.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can show how to widen options: involving the person, timing, partial sharing, consultation, and doing nothing yet.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when every option is bad: choose the least harmful, and record why.",
"error": "Framing every dilemma as a choice between two options.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Evaluate the likely outcome of each'.",
"evidence":"Appendix 8 dilemma entry · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Work an ethical dilemma through a decision procedure","Evaluate the likely outcome of each"): {
"why":   "Step 5 of Appendix A is to evaluate the likely consequences of each option for each party. This is where the earlier steps pay off: parties, rights and options meet. Thinking consequences through — short and long term, for each person — often shows that the option that feels most ethical in the moment has costs you had not seen, or the reverse.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor tests each option: 'what happens next if we do this — and then what?' Notice whether they think past the first reaction.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the consequences they name for each option, for whom, and over what time frame.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'which consequence do trainees most often miss?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — drafting the consequences of each option for each party, in a grid, short and long term.\n\nTHEY HOLD — correcting your predictions from what they have seen happen in similar cases.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — predicting consequences improves with experience, so theirs corrects yours. The grid makes your predictions visible so they can be corrected.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — present the consequence grid and your recommendation in supervision, and defend it.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — short-term thinking (what calms today's meeting), and consequences for the child left out because the adults were louder.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'and six months from now? What does the child experience?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — for each option, think through short and long-term consequences for each party, and write them down.\n\nPASS TEST — Consequences thought through\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — Appendix 8 dilemma entry. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a decision that solved the immediate problem and damaged the relationship with the family for the rest of the involvement.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can show how to lay out a consequence grid — options by parties by time frame — and how to test predictions against experience.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when consequences cannot be predicted: choose the most reversible option, set a date to review, and record that uncertainty was part of the decision.",
"error": "Choosing the option that relieves your discomfort now rather than the one with the best consequences.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Decide and record the decision'.",
"evidence":"Appendix 8 dilemma entry · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Work an ethical dilemma through a decision procedure","Decide and record the decision"): {
"why":   "Step 6 of Appendix A is to make the decision. Recording it — what was decided, why, and which options were rejected — lets you, your supervisor, or a reviewer see that the decision was reasoned. It also makes it defensible if challenged. A decision reached in conversation and never written down is, for any later purpose, a decision nobody can show was made.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how and where your supervisor records an ethical decision: the case file, their supervision record, or both — and whether they record options they rejected.\n\nWRITE DOWN — what the record contains: decision, reasoning, Code clauses, options rejected, who was consulted, date.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'have you ever needed to show your reasoning afterwards?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — writing up the decision for a dilemma your supervisor decided, including the reasons for rejecting the other options.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the decision itself, and checking your write-up is accurate.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — writing up someone else's decision tests whether you followed the reasoning, without you carrying the consequence.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — make the decision on your own case and write it up, with your supervisor agreeing it.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — recording the decision and not the reasoning.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'would someone reading this know why you didn't choose option two?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — decide, and record the decision, the reasoning and the options rejected, on the day.\n\nPASS TEST — Written down\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — case record and Appendix 8 dilemma entry. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a challenge months later and no record of why you did what you did.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain what a decision record needs and why the rejected options matter: they show the decision was a choice, not a default.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you did not record at the time: write it now, dated, clearly marked as written later, and say so if asked.",
"error": "Recording what was decided but not why.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Take responsibility for the consequences' and 'Keep notes at each stage'.",
"evidence":"Case record · Appendix 8 dilemma entry · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Work an ethical dilemma through a decision procedure","Take responsibility for the consequences"): {
"why":   "Step 7 of Appendix A is to take responsibility for the decision and its consequences, and to act to correct them where they are harmful. A decision made through a good procedure can still go badly. What matters then is honesty: saying so, correcting what can be corrected, and learning from it. Defending a bad outcome because the procedure was followed misses the point of having a procedure.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor talks about a decision that went badly: whether they own it, explain it away, or blame the system.\n\nWRITE DOWN — what they did afterwards: who they told, what they corrected, what they changed in their practice.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'tell me about a decision you'd make differently now, and what you did once you knew.'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — reviewing the outcome of a decision, and naming anything that went wrong.\n\nTHEY HOLD — deciding on corrective action.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — naming what went wrong is the habit. What to do about it may involve the family, the school or the service.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — review your own decision's outcome in supervision, name what went wrong, and propose corrective action.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — defensiveness, blaming the procedure or the family, and treating 'I followed the steps' as the end of the matter.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'setting aside what others did, what's yours in this?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — review the outcome, own the part that was yours, and act to correct harm.\n\nPASS TEST — Including where it went wrong\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — Appendix 8 follow-up and supervision record. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a harmful outcome explained away as the right decision on the information available, with nothing done about it.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain step 7, and model owning a decision that went wrong.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when the consequence is serious: tell your supervisor at once, follow service procedure, and consider whether it needs to be reported.",
"error": "Defending a bad outcome because the procedure was followed.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Keep notes at each stage' and into Supervision.",
"evidence":"Appendix 8 follow-up · supervision record · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Work an ethical dilemma through a decision procedure","Keep notes at each stage"): {
"why":   "The pass test for this micro-skill records that the PSI Code strongly recommends keeping notes throughout the decision-making procedure. Read the Appendix A wording yourself. Notes at each stage show the reasoning as it developed, not as reconstructed after the outcome is known. They protect you if challenged, and they are the raw material for your Appendix 8 dilemma entries.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — whether your supervisor keeps notes as a dilemma unfolds, or only records the outcome once it is settled.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the headings they use, and where the notes are kept (case file, supervision record, or a separate log).\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'where do you keep these notes, and who can see them?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — keeping dated, stage-by-stage notes for a dilemma your supervisor is working through, one heading per step of Appendix A.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the decision and the case record.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — note-keeping is a habit you can build on someone else's decision first, so it is automatic by the time the decision is yours.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — keep your own stage-by-stage notes on a dilemma in your case, and bring them to supervision at each step rather than at the end.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — notes written all at once, after the event, which read as if you knew the answer from the start.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'when did you write this part?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — keep dated notes at each of the seven steps as you go.\n\nPASS TEST — The Code strongly recommends it\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — Appendix 8 dilemma entry. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — an Appendix 8 entry written from memory weeks later, which reads as if you knew the answer from the start.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can show a peer a simple seven-step note template, and explain why contemporaneous notes carry weight.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when notes are missing for a stage: write what you remember, dated and marked as later.",
"error": "Writing the whole dilemma record after the outcome is known.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into Appendix 8 and into Supervision.",
"evidence":"Appendix 8 dilemma entry · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

# ── 6. ETHICAL PRACTICE · State and hold the limits of your competence ──

("State and hold the limits of your competence","Know your publisher competence level for every tool"): {
"why":   "Publishers restrict each instrument to users with particular qualifications, and label them by level in their catalogues. The level systems differ between publishers and between the UK/Ireland and US catalogues, so check each tool rather than assuming. PSI Code 3.3.6 is the clause mapped here — not to be confused with PSI accreditation criterion 3.3.6 on placement settings; read the Code clause itself before citing it. As a trainee you usually use restricted tools under supervision, and the qualification rests with your supervisor. If you cannot say which tools need which level, you cannot tell when you are working beyond what you are entitled to do.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor talks about restricted tools: which ones they say a trainee can give under supervision, and which they reserve.\n\nWRITE DOWN — every tool in the service's cupboard, with the publisher and the qualification level printed in that publisher's catalogue.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'which tools here would you never let a trainee give without you in the room, and is that the publisher's rule or yours?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — building a table of tools, publisher levels, and your status for each (not trained, trained and supervised, cleared by placement).\n\nTHEY HOLD — confirming the table, and the service's own rules on top.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — looking up levels is research you can do accurately yourself. Placement clearance is their decision under their qualification.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — keep the table current and consult it before offering to use any tool.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — 'I've done a WISC before' used as if it answered the question for another test or another setting.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'what level is that one, and who holds it for your use here?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — for any tool, state the publisher's level, who holds the qualification for your use, and what supervision is agreed.\n\nPASS TEST — You can state it\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the tools table alongside the Placement Plan. Log against 6. Ethical Practice and 1. Assessment.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — administering a restricted instrument with no agreed supervision, so the scores may not be defensible and the service is exposed.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain how publisher levels work, why they exist, and how trainee use sits under a qualified supervisor.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you find you used a tool beyond your agreed level: tell your supervisor, and decide together whether the scores can be reported and how.",
"error": "Treating experience with one tool as clearance for a different tool.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Ask which tools you may administer independently here' and 'Say early what you are not yet competent to do'.",
"evidence":"Tools table with Placement Plan · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice and 1. Assessment"},

("State and hold the limits of your competence","Say early what you are not yet competent to do"): {
"why":   "Supervisors allocate work based on what they think you can do. If you do not tell them where you are not yet competent, they will assume you can, and the gap shows up in front of a family or school. CORU SoP 1.2 and PSI Code 2.2.2 are the clauses mapped here. Saying it early, at allocation or in the Placement Plan, is professional. Saying it after the work has gone wrong is too late, and no one can fix the problem in time.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor talks about what they do not do: referrals they pass on, tools they have not used, areas they consult on.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the words they use to say it, without apologising or over-explaining.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what do you wish trainees told you earlier?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — writing your own list of areas where you are not yet competent: tools, age bands, presenting problems, types of meeting.\n\nTHEY HOLD — how the work is allocated around that list.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — honest self-assessment is yours. Allocation is theirs, and they can only do it well with your list.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — raise competence gaps at allocation, before accepting work, with your supervisor responding.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — accepting everything to look capable, and flagging gaps only once work is under way.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'is there anything in this case you haven't done before?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — state competence gaps at allocation, without being asked, and propose how the gap will be covered (co-working, supervision, training).\n\nPASS TEST — Before being asked\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — supervision record and Placement Plan. Log against 6. Ethical Practice and 7. Supervision.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — you lead a feedback meeting on a presentation you have never worked with, and cannot answer the parent's questions.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain why early disclosure of gaps is a professional strength, and how to phrase it with a plan attached.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you find a gap mid-case: say so straight away, and agree with your supervisor how to cover it.",
"error": "Accepting work beyond your competence to avoid looking less capable.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Decline work that exceeds you' and into Supervision.",
"evidence":"Supervision record · Placement Plan · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice and 7. Supervision"},

("State and hold the limits of your competence","Decline work that exceeds you"): {
"why":   "Sometimes flagging a gap is not enough, and the right answer is no. A request might be far beyond your training — a complex risk assessment, a court report, a presentation you have no grounding in — or no adequate supervision may be available. PSI Code 2.2.3 is the clause mapped here. Declining protects the child from work that is not good enough. A trainee who says yes to everything is a risk to the people they work with.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor declines work: to a school, a colleague, a manager. Notice the words and whether they offer an alternative.\n\nWRITE DOWN — a phrase you could use yourself.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'when did you last say no to a piece of work, and how did it land?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — identifying requests that exceed you, and saying so to your supervisor.\n\nTHEY HOLD — declining on your behalf to the school or service, while you are learning how.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — recognising the limit is yours. Saying no to a principal on a live placement involves relationships your supervisor manages.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — decline work yourself, to the person asking, with an alternative, and your supervisor informed.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — saying yes and then quietly struggling, or declining without offering any alternative.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'would it help to say you can do the observation but not the report?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — decline work that exceeds you, clearly and courteously, with an alternative where possible, and record it.\n\nPASS TEST — You declined\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — case note or email, and supervision record. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a report written beyond your competence, which then has to be withdrawn or corrected.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain when declining is required rather than optional, and how to do it while keeping the relationship.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you are pressured to accept: repeat the limit, explain the risk to the child, and escalate to your supervisor.",
"error": "Saying yes to work beyond you because the school needs it done.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Refer on and maintain responsibility until contact is made'.",
"evidence":"Case note or email · supervision record · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("State and hold the limits of your competence","Refer on and maintain responsibility until contact is made"): {
"why":   "Referral is a handover, not an exit. Until the receiving service has made contact, the child is still yours to hold in mind. Referrals get lost, rejected, or sit on waiting lists while risk changes. PSI Code 3.4.3 is the clause mapped here. A trainee who closes the file the day the referral letter goes has left the child in a gap between services. That gap is exactly where harm can go unnoticed.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — what your supervisor does after sending a referral: whether they diarise a follow-up, and what they tell the family and school in the meantime.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the steps from referral sent to referral accepted and contact made.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'have you had a referral go missing? How did you find out?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — writing the referral, confirming it was received, and diarising a follow-up date to check contact has been made.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the decision to refer and when to close.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — writing the referral and tracking it are practical. Closing the case carries responsibility for what happens after.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — make the referral, follow it up, and keep the family informed, with your supervisor overseeing.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — closing the file when the letter goes, and no follow-up date.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'has CAMHS acknowledged it yet? When will you check?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — refer, confirm receipt, follow up until the receiving service has made contact, and only then hand over.\n\nPASS TEST — You did not drop it at referral\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the referral, acknowledgement and follow-up notes. Log against 6. Ethical Practice and 5. Working with Others.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a family who waited months for a service that never received the referral.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain why responsibility continues after referral and show a simple tracking method.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when a referral is lost or rejected: tell the family, re-refer or find another route, and record the gap.",
"error": "Closing the case the day the referral letter is posted.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Learn the referral and risk pathways' and into Working with Others.",
"evidence":"Referral · acknowledgement · follow-up notes · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice and 5. Working with Others"},

("State and hold the limits of your competence","Include a limitations paragraph in every report"): {
"why":   "A limitations paragraph is where you state the limits of your own competence and your evidence in writing. CORU SoP 1.2 is the standard mapped here. It sits in Ethical Practice as well as Assessment because it is the public form of knowing your limits. Every report has some. A report that states none is either unusually complete or not being honest, and readers cannot tell which. This micro-skill is about consistency — every report, not only the difficult ones.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — whether every report your supervisor writes has a limitations paragraph, including the straightforward ones.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the limitations stated in three of their reports, and how each is specific to that case.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'do you ever write a report without one?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — drafting the limitations paragraph for every report you contribute to.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the final wording.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — making it a habit on every draft is what builds consistency. Wording that the family will read is still theirs to approve.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — write limitations for each of your own reports, and have your supervisor check them for specificity.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — the same paragraph pasted into every report.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'this is the same wording as your last report. What's specific to this child?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — every report has a specific limitations paragraph, including your own competence limits where relevant.\n\nPASS TEST — Every one\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the reports. Keep a tally in your Log. Log against 6. Ethical Practice and 1. Assessment.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — one report in five without a limitations paragraph, and that is the one that gets challenged.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain why a limitations paragraph is an ethical requirement, not just an assessment convention, and show what makes one specific.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when a report went out without one: issue an addendum if the missing limits matter to the conclusions.",
"error": "Pasting the same generic limitations paragraph into every report.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'State what you did not assess and what that limits' and into report quality throughout.",
"evidence":"Reports · Log tally · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice and 1. Assessment"},

# ── 6. ETHICAL PRACTICE · Manage workload and your own wellbeing ──

("Manage workload and your own wellbeing","Track your actual caseload and travel load"): {
"why":   "Educational psychology in rural Ireland involves long drives between schools. A caseload that looks manageable on paper can be unmanageable once travel, report-writing and meetings are counted. CORU SoP 1.18 is the standard mapped here. Without the numbers you cannot raise workload credibly — 'I'm overwhelmed' is easier to dismiss than 'I have twelve open cases, eleven hours' driving a week, and six reports due'. The Cavan placement (05/10/2026–18/12/2026, four days a week) is where to start counting.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor tracks their own workload: a spreadsheet, a diary, a whiteboard. Notice whether they count travel and writing time.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the categories they track.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what's a realistic caseload for a trainee here, and how would you know I was over it?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — keeping a weekly record: open cases, sessions, meetings, reports due, travel hours, and hours worked.\n\nTHEY HOLD — deciding what is a reasonable load.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — keeping the numbers is yours. Judging what is reasonable needs knowledge of the service and of trainee norms.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — bring your numbers to supervision each week, with a one-line summary of how the load is.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — numbers kept but not looked at, and travel and writing time left out.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'you've got four reports due next week and two days in schools. When are you writing?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — keep a weekly workload record that includes travel and writing, and know your numbers without looking.\n\nPASS TEST — You know the numbers\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the weekly record, which can sit in the Log. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — you realise in week nine that four reports are overdue and you did not see it coming.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can show a peer a simple workload tracker and explain why travel and writing time are where caseloads hide.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when the numbers show you are over capacity: take them to supervision and ask for re-prioritisation, not more effort.",
"error": "Counting cases but not travel or report-writing time.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Raise workload before it becomes a problem'.",
"evidence":"Weekly workload record · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Manage workload and your own wellbeing","Recognise your own warning signs"): {
"why":   "Burnout is described as emotional exhaustion, depersonalisation and reduced personal accomplishment (Maslach & Jackson, Journal of Occupational Behaviour, 1981). It builds slowly, and the person inside it is often the last to see it. Everyone's early signs are different: sleep, irritability, dread before work, cynicism about families, cutting corners on records. CORU SoP 5.45 is the standard mapped here. Knowing your own signs is what lets you act before your judgement is affected.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor talks about their own stress, if they do, and what they do to manage it.\n\nWRITE DOWN — for yourself, privately, what you notice in yourself when stretched: body, mood, thinking, behaviour.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what are your early signs, and what do you do when you notice them?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — naming your warning signs, privately, and deciding which you will share in supervision.\n\nTHEY HOLD — noticing if they see signs you have not mentioned.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — your signs are yours to know. A supervisor is a second pair of eyes, not a monitor.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — check your signs weekly, and raise it in supervision when they appear.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — a trainee who says they are fine while their records slip and their language about families turns cynical.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'you seem stretched. How are you actually doing?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — name your warning signs, check them regularly, and act on them.\n\nPASS TEST — You can name them\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — nothing personal needs filing. A supervision note that wellbeing was discussed is enough. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — you notice at the end of placement that you were running on empty for weeks, and your later reports show it.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain burnout and its early signs, citing sources, and help a peer identify their own.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when the signs are there and not easing: tell your supervisor, use the employee assistance or university counselling service, and see your GP.",
"error": "Noticing your signs and deciding to push through until the placement ends.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Seek supervision when stressed, not only when stuck' and 'Refrain from practice where judgement is seriously impaired'.",
"evidence":"Supervision record · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Manage workload and your own wellbeing","Raise workload before it becomes a problem"): {
"why":   "Workload raised early can be solved by re-prioritising, deferring or re-allocating. Workload raised after deadlines are missed becomes a performance conversation, and families and schools have already been let down. CORU SoP 1.18 is the standard mapped here. Trainees often hold back because they fear looking less capable than their peers. Supervisors consistently prefer to hear early.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor raises workload with their own manager: what they bring and what they ask for.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the structure: the numbers, the pinch point, the specific ask.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what's the earliest point you'd want me to tell you I was stretched?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — spotting the pinch point in your workload record and naming it.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the decision about what to defer or re-allocate.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — spotting the problem is yours. Re-prioritising involves other people's work and service commitments.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — raise a workload issue before a deadline is at risk, with the numbers and a proposal.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — waiting until you are already behind, and raising it as a general complaint with no proposal.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'what would you take off your list if you could?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — raise workload as soon as your numbers show a pinch point ahead, with a specific proposal.\n\nPASS TEST — Before, not after\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — supervision record. Log against 6. Ethical Practice and 7. Supervision.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — you raise it the day after a report deadline was missed.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain why early is better, and how to raise workload with numbers and a proposal.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when raising it did not change anything: raise it again, in writing, and involve the university placement tutor if needed.",
"error": "Raising workload only after a deadline has been missed.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Seek supervision when stressed, not only when stuck' and into Supervision.",
"evidence":"Supervision record · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice and 7. Supervision"},

("Manage workload and your own wellbeing","Seek supervision when stressed, not only when stuck"): {
"why":   "Supervision is not only for case problems. PSI Code 4.1.2 is the clause mapped here, and the pass test records that seeking support is required, not merely permitted. Stress affects judgement, record-keeping and how you relate to families, so it is a practice issue, not only a personal one. Trainees often bring cases to supervision and keep stress to themselves. That leaves the supervisor managing risks they cannot see.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — whether your supervisor's supervision sessions have space for wellbeing, and how it is raised.\n\nWRITE DOWN — how the agenda is set, and whether wellbeing is a standing item.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'how do you use your own supervision when you're stressed?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — adding wellbeing to your supervision agenda as a standing item, and saying one honest sentence under it each week, even when it is 'steady'.\n\nTHEY HOLD — creating space for it, and responding.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — raising it is yours; the supervisor cannot respond to what they do not know, and a standing item makes it ordinary rather than an alarm.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — raise stress in supervision when it is there, and link it to its effect on your practice.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — 'fine' every week, and stress raised only as an excuse after something has gone wrong.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'you said fine. On a scale of one to ten?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — bring stress to supervision when it is present, not only case problems.\n\nPASS TEST — It is required, not permitted\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — supervision record noting wellbeing discussed. Log against 6. Ethical Practice and 7. Supervision.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — your supervisor learns you were struggling from your placement evaluation, not from you.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain why stress is a practice issue and how to raise it in supervision without it becoming therapy.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when supervision is not a safe place to raise it: use the university placement tutor or another route.",
"error": "Bringing only case problems to supervision and keeping stress to yourself.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Refrain from practice where judgement is seriously impaired' and into Supervision.",
"evidence":"Supervision record · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice and 7. Supervision"},

("Manage workload and your own wellbeing","Know the seven-day notification duty"): {
"why":   "The UCD Handbook §5 sets out a seven-day notification duty. Its exact triggers and route were not in the sources used to write this row, so do not act on a summary. Read §5 and write down, in your own words, what triggers it, who you notify, how, and within what time. Duties like this usually cover changes in circumstances that bear on fitness to practise. Missing the deadline can become a professional issue in itself, whatever the underlying matter was.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — whether your supervisor or placement tutor mentions notification duties at induction.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the exact wording of Handbook §5: what triggers it, who is notified, how, and the time limit.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — ask your placement tutor, 'can you give me an example of something that would trigger the seven-day duty?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — reading §5 and summarising it in your own words on one page.\n\nTHEY HOLD — (placement tutor) confirming your summary is correct.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — reading the rule is yours. Confirming your reading avoids acting on a misunderstanding when it matters.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — keep your summary where you can find it, and check a hypothetical scenario against it with your tutor.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — assuming the duty only covers serious matters, and not knowing how to notify.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'if that happened on a Friday, when would the seven days end?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — know the trigger, the recipient, the method and the deadline, and act within it if needed.\n\nPASS TEST — You could act on it if needed\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — your one-page summary of §5, kept in your portfolio. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — something triggers the duty and you learn about the deadline after it has passed.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain the duty to a new trainee from the Handbook text, not from memory.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you missed the deadline: notify at once, explain the delay honestly, and tell your placement tutor.",
"error": "Not reading §5 until something triggers it.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Refrain from practice where judgement is seriously impaired'.",
"evidence":"One-page summary of Handbook §5 · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

("Manage workload and your own wellbeing","Refrain from practice where judgement is seriously impaired"): {
"why":   "Illness, bereavement, exhaustion, medication, alcohol, or a crisis at home can all seriously impair judgement. PSI Code 2.5.1 is the clause mapped here: a psychologist whose judgement is seriously impaired should refrain from practice. The difficulty is that impairment affects the judgement you would use to notice it. So the skill is knowing your signs in advance, having an agreed plan, and accepting a supervisor's view when they see it before you do.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how the service handles a psychologist who is unwell or in crisis: cover arrangements, how cases are protected, and whether it is treated as normal.\n\nWRITE DOWN — who you would tell, and how your cases would be covered.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'have you ever stepped back from work? How did you know it was time?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — agreeing in advance with your supervisor what would lead you to step back, and who covers.\n\nTHEY HOLD — the decision if you are not able to make it yourself.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — a plan made while well is the only reliable one, because impaired judgement is poor at judging itself.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — if impaired, say so, step back from direct work, and hand over cases per the plan.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — pushing on because of placement days or deadlines, and seeing families while unwell.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'I think you need to go home. I'll cover this afternoon.'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — recognise serious impairment, stop direct practice, tell your supervisor and tutor, and follow any notification duty.\n\nPASS TEST — You would know and would act\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — nothing personal needs filing. Supervision record of the plan. Log against 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a feedback meeting led while seriously unwell, with a family given confused or inaccurate information.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain the duty, why a pre-agreed plan matters, and why stepping back is professional rather than a failure.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you realise afterwards that you practised while impaired: tell your supervisor, review the affected work, and correct anything that needs it.",
"error": "Continuing to see families while seriously unwell because placement days are needed.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into Supervision, and into 'Know the seven-day notification duty'.",
"evidence":"Supervision record of plan · Log sheet tagged 6. Ethical Practice"},

# ── 2. FORMULATION · Learn the four frameworks for practice ──

("Learn the four frameworks for practice","Read all four frameworks named in the handbook"): {
"why":   "Handbook §2.1 names four frameworks: Monsen's Problem-Analysis Framework (Monsen & Frederickson, 2008), COMOIRA (Gameson & Rhydderch, 2008), the Interactive Factors Framework (Frederickson & Cline, 2009, after Morton & Frith), and Carr's model (Carr, 2015). The first two are chapters in Kelly, Woolfson & Boyle (Eds.), Frameworks for Practice in Educational Psychology (2008). A trainee who has not read them does not work without a framework. They work with an implicit one, usually a within-child deficit model picked up from report templates, and cannot defend it when asked.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — which framework your supervisor's reports reflect, even if unnamed: hypotheses with evidence for and against (Monsen), levels with arrows (IFF), competing accounts held side by side (COMOIRA), or predisposing/precipitating/perpetuating/protective factors (Carr).\n\nWRITE DOWN — for each of three reports, which framework you think it reflects and the sentence that told you.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'did you train in one of the four, or did you come to your approach another way?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — reading all four in the original chapters, not summaries, and writing one page on each: what it foregrounds, its stages, what it leaves out, what referral it suits.\n\nTHEY HOLD — correcting your reading where it misrepresents a framework.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — reading is yours and cannot be delegated. A supervisor's check catches the common misreadings, such as treating COMOIRA as 'just consultation'.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — explain each framework in supervision in two minutes, without notes, and say what it foregrounds.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — descriptions that make the four sound interchangeable, and an account of Monsen with no competing hypotheses in it.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'what does COMOIRA put at the centre that Monsen doesn't?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — name all four, their authors and sources, and state in a sentence what each foregrounds.\n\nPASS TEST — You can name what each foregrounds\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — your four one-page summaries, kept in your portfolio. Log against 2. Formulation.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — at a viva or examiner's question you can name the frameworks but not say how they differ.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can teach a first-year trainee the four frameworks in one session, with a worked case run through each, citing the original chapters.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you realise you have been working from an implicit within-child model: re-read one current case through a named framework and see what changes.",
"error": "Reading summaries of the frameworks instead of the chapters, so the stages are remembered but not the reasoning.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Identify what each framework is good for', 'Identify what each leaves out', and 'Choose a framework deliberately for this case'.",
"evidence":"Four one-page summaries · Log sheet tagged 2. Formulation"},

("Learn the four frameworks for practice","Identify what each framework is good for"): {
"why":   "Each framework suits different referrals. The Reference sheet (Part C) sets it out: Monsen for contested questions and disagreeing informants; the IFF for multi-level developmental and learning presentations; COMOIRA where the adults hold incompatible views and consultation leads; Carr where onset and maintenance matter, such as school avoidance or emerging low mood. If you cannot match framework to referral, you cannot choose, and you will default.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — the referral types your supervisor gets, and how they approach each: which ones they treat as hypothesis-testing, which as consultation, which as developmental.\n\nWRITE DOWN — for each case you see, the referral type and the framework you think would have suited it best.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'which kind of referral do you find hardest to formulate, and why?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — building a table: referral types down the side, the four frameworks across, with a note on fit for each.\n\nTHEY HOLD — correcting fit from their experience of real cases.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — the table is reasoning you can do from reading. Experience is what shows where a framework looks right on paper and fails in practice.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — for each new referral, say in supervision which framework you think fits and why, before any data is gathered.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — the same answer for every referral, or a choice justified by preference rather than by the referral.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'the parents and school disagree completely here. Does that change your choice?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — match each referral to a framework on the basis of what the referral is: contested, multi-level, a disagreement among adults, or about onset and maintenance.\n\nPASS TEST — You can say which referral type suits which\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the fit table in your portfolio, and a line in each case record naming the choice. Log against 2. Formulation.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — Carr's model applied to a whole-class behaviour consultation, producing an individual formulation of a systemic problem.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can give a worked referral for each framework and explain why it fits better than the other three.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when a case changes character midway, such as a learning referral that becomes a disagreement among adults: say so, and switch framework openly rather than bending the first one.",
"error": "Choosing a framework because you like it rather than because the referral calls for it.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Choose a framework deliberately for this case' and 'Identify what each leaves out'.",
"evidence":"Fit table · case records · Log sheet tagged 2. Formulation"},

("Learn the four frameworks for practice","Identify what each leaves out"): {
"why":   "Every framework has a blind spot, and knowing yours is what PSI Code 4.2.6 asks of you here. The Reference sheet names them: Monsen can tidy a messy situation by dropping hypotheses that did not resolve; the IFF's arrows are easy to draw and hard to justify, since each is a causal claim; COMOIRA can drift away from the data; Carr's model centres the individual and can under-weight the classroom. If you cannot say what your preferred framework misses, you will miss it too, every time.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — moments where your supervisor's formulation seems to leave something out: the classroom in an individual formulation, or the child's view in a hypothesis-testing one.\n\nWRITE DOWN — what was left out, and which framework feature made it easy to leave out.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what does your usual approach tend to miss?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — writing, for each framework, one sentence on what it leaves out and one example of a case where that would matter.\n\nTHEY HOLD — correcting your examples.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — critique of frameworks is part of your reading. Examples from real cases are where supervisor experience adds most.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — for your own formulation, state in supervision what the chosen framework is likely to have missed in this case, and what you did about it.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — a framework treated as complete, and a limitation stated in general terms but not checked against this case.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'you used Carr. Where's the classroom in this?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — name the limitation of the framework you used, check whether it affected this case, and say so in the limitations paragraph.\n\nPASS TEST — You can name a limitation of your preferred one\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the limitations paragraph of the report. Log against 2. Formulation.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — an IFF diagram with arrows nobody can justify, and no sentence acknowledging that they are hypotheses.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain each framework's blind spot, give a case where it matters, and show how to compensate for it.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when the blind spot has shaped a finished formulation: revisit it using a second framework as a check, and revise if the second view shows something the first missed.",
"error": "Treating your preferred framework as if it had no blind spot.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Make your preferred framework explicit in supervision' and into the limitations paragraph.",
"evidence":"Report — limitations · Log sheet tagged 2. Formulation"},

("Learn the four frameworks for practice","Make your preferred framework explicit in supervision"): {
"why":   "Everyone has a default. Stating yours in supervision lets your supervisor watch for the blind spot that comes with it, and challenge you when the default does not fit the case. Handbook §6 and CORU SoP 5.44 are the references mapped here — read §6 for the programme's expectation of supervision. An unstated default is invisible to both of you, so neither can correct it.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor talks about their own theoretical orientation, and whether they do.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the framework you notice yourself reaching for first when you read a new referral.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what's my default, from what you've seen of my work so far?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — naming your default framework in supervision, with an honest reason why it is your default: training, comfort, or genuine fit.\n\nTHEY HOLD — watching for when the default is being applied where it does not fit.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — only you can name your default. Spotting its misuse needs an outside eye.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — state your default at the start of placement, and each time you choose a framework, say whether this is the default or a deliberate departure.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — naming a default and then never departing from it.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'that's your default again. Did you consider the others?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — your supervisor knows your default without asking, and you flag every case where you use it.\n\nPASS TEST — Your supervisor knows what you default to\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — supervision record. Log against 2. Formulation and 7. Supervision.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — at your mid-placement review, your supervisor notes that every formulation used the same framework, and you had not noticed.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain why defaults are inevitable, and how stating them in supervision turns them from a blind spot into something checkable.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when you find you have defaulted on every case: take the last three cases and re-formulate one with a different framework, in supervision.",
"error": "Never naming your default, so no one can spot where it misleads you.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Ask your supervisor which they use and why' and into Supervision.",
"evidence":"Supervision record · Log sheet tagged 2. Formulation and 7. Supervision"},

("Learn the four frameworks for practice","Ask your supervisor which they use and why"): {
"why":   "Supervisors have defaults too, and yours shapes how your reports will be read and corrected. Knowing whether your supervisor was trained in a framework, or came to their approach through experience, tells you what they will look for and where they are likely to push back. CORU SoP 4.5 is the standard mapped here. It is a legitimate question, and most supervisors welcome it.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — the framework your supervisor's reports and case discussions reflect.\n\nWRITE DOWN — your guess at their default, before you ask.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'which framework do you use most, and were you trained in it, or did you come to it another way?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — asking the question in an early supervision session, and noting the answer.\n\nTHEY HOLD — their answer, and how much they want to discuss it.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — asking is yours, and it signals that you are thinking about frameworks rather than just filling in a report. How far to take the conversation is theirs.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — use what you know of your supervisor's framework to anticipate their feedback, and say so: 'I know you'd usually look at this through X; I've used Y because…'\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — adopting their framework to please them rather than because it fits.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'don't use mine because it's mine.'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — know your supervisor's framework, whether they trained in it, and how it shapes their reading of your work.\n\nPASS TEST — You know whether they were trained in it\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — supervision record. Log against 2. Formulation and 7. Supervision.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — repeated feedback you do not understand, because you and your supervisor are working from different frameworks without knowing it.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain why knowing a supervisor's framework matters, and how to raise it without it sounding like a challenge.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when your frameworks conflict on a case: name the difference in supervision and agree which fits this referral, with reasons.",
"error": "Taking on your supervisor's framework by imitation, without knowing that is what you are doing.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into Supervision and into 'Choose a framework deliberately for this case'.",
"evidence":"Supervision record · Log sheet tagged 2. Formulation and 7. Supervision"},

# ── 2. FORMULATION · Name and apply one framework to each substantial case ──

("Name and apply one framework to each substantial case","Choose a framework deliberately for this case"): {
"why":   "The referral question should choose the framework, not habit. The Reference sheet gives the test: is the difficulty contested (Monsen), multi-level and developmental (IFF), a disagreement between adults (COMOIRA), or about onset and maintenance (Carr)? Choose before collecting data, because the framework decides what data you need. A framework chosen after the data is in is retrospective justification. An examiner will see that immediately.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — when in the process your supervisor settles on an approach: at referral, after the first meeting, or only at write-up.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the referral, the framework, and the point at which it was chosen.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'what in this referral told you which framework to use?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — proposing the framework for a case at referral, in writing, with one line of reasoning.\n\nTHEY HOLD — agreeing or redirecting the choice.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — making the choice explicit is yours. Their agreement protects the case while your judgement is calibrating.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — choose the framework at referral, plan the data collection from it, and defend the choice in supervision.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — a choice made after the data, and a data plan that does not follow from the framework.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'if you're using Monsen, what are your competing hypotheses before you go in?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — choose the framework at referral, write the reason down, and plan data collection from it.\n\nPASS TEST — You can say why this one and not another\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — case record noting framework and reason, and the report. Log against 2. Formulation.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a report that names Monsen but was clearly written first and framed afterwards.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain how the referral chooses the framework, with worked examples of each choice.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when new information shows the framework was wrong: say so, change it, and record why, rather than forcing the data into the original choice.",
"error": "Choosing the framework after writing the report, to fit what you found.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Name and cite it in the report' and 'Use the framework's own structure'.",
"evidence":"Case record · report · Log sheet tagged 2. Formulation"},

("Name and apply one framework to each substantial case","Name and cite it in the report"): {
"why":   "Naming and citing the framework tells the reader how to read your formulation, and lets them check it against the source. The Reference sheet calls naming the framework in the report 'the marker' for Year 1 formulation. It also shows your reasoning rests on a published model rather than personal judgement alone. A report that uses a framework without naming it hides its structure. One that names a framework without using it is worse.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — whether your supervisor names a framework in their reports, and where: the method section, the formulation heading, or not at all.\n\nWRITE DOWN — how it is cited, and whether the citation is APA 7th.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'do schools and parents find the framework name helpful, or is it for other psychologists?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — adding the framework name and a full APA 7th citation to the draft, in the method and formulation sections.\n\nTHEY HOLD — deciding how much explanation of the framework the audience needs.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — naming and citing is mechanical. Pitching it for a parent or teacher readership is judgement.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — name, cite, and briefly explain the framework in plain language in each report.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — a name dropped in the introduction with no sign of the framework anywhere else.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'you cite IFF. Where are the levels?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — name the framework, cite it in APA 7th, and explain it in one plain sentence for the reader.\n\nPASS TEST — It appears by name with a citation\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the report. Log against 2. Formulation.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a formulation an examiner cannot place in any framework, or one that names a framework it does not follow.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain why naming the framework matters to different readers, and how to explain each in one plain sentence.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when a parent asks what the framework means: explain it simply in terms of what you looked at and why.",
"error": "Citing a framework decoratively, with nothing in the report that uses it.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Use the framework's own structure'.",
"evidence":"Report — method and formulation · Log sheet tagged 2. Formulation"},

("Name and apply one framework to each substantial case","Use the framework's own structure"): {
"why":   "A framework you name should shape the report. The Reference sheet's test: delete the framework's name from your report — could a reader still tell which one you used? If you chose Monsen, there is a hypotheses section with evidence for and against. If IFF, there are levels and stated interactions. If Carr, there are predisposing, precipitating, perpetuating and protective factors. If COMOIRA, the different accounts are held side by side with a focus on change.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how the structure of your supervisor's reports follows, or does not follow, a framework.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the headings of the formulation section, and which framework stage each corresponds to.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'do you adapt the framework's structure for school readers, and how?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — drafting the formulation section using the framework's stages as headings or clear signposts.\n\nTHEY HOLD — how much of the structure is visible in the version sent to the family.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — using the structure is the discipline you need to build. How visible to make it for a lay reader is a judgement about audience.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — write the formulation following the framework's stages, and walk your supervisor through it stage by stage.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — a standard report with a framework sentence bolted on.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'take the name out. Which framework is this?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — write every formulation so that the framework's stages are visible to a reader who knows the framework.\n\nPASS TEST — The stages are visible rather than implied\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the report. Log against 2. Formulation.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a formulation that could have been written with any framework, or none.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can show how each framework's structure appears in a report, with an example for each.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when the structure makes the report hard for a parent to follow: keep the structure in the formulation section, and write a plain summary for the family.",
"error": "Writing a standard report and adding a framework sentence to the introduction.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Show the reasoning step by step'.",
"evidence":"Report — formulation · Log sheet tagged 2. Formulation"},

("Name and apply one framework to each substantial case","Show the reasoning step by step"): {
"why":   "A conclusion without visible reasoning cannot be checked or challenged. It can only be accepted or rejected whole. CORU SoP 3.5 and PSI Code 4.2.5 (distinguish facts, opinions, theories and hypotheses) are the standards mapped here. The Reference sheet's worked example: not 'John has a specific learning difficulty' but his percentile scores, what rules out instruction and attendance, and the most parsimonious remaining explanation. The second version can be argued with — that is the point.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor moves from data to conclusion in writing: whether each inference is stated or left to the reader.\n\nWRITE DOWN — one paragraph where the reasoning is visible, and one where it is not.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'when a parent challenges a conclusion, what do you point them to?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — drafting a formulation paragraph as a chain: finding → what it rules out → what remains → conclusion.\n\nTHEY HOLD — testing each link.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — writing the chain exposes your reasoning. Testing it needs someone who can spot the weak link.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — write the full formulation as visible reasoning and present it in supervision.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — conclusions stated as fact, and inference steps skipped because they felt obvious.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'how did you get from that score to that conclusion?'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — write each conclusion with its evidence, what it rules out, and why the conclusion is the best remaining explanation.\n\nPASS TEST — Someone could point at where they disagree\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the formulation section. Log against 2. Formulation.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a parent disputes a conclusion, and the report gives you nothing to point to except the conclusion itself.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can show a peer how to turn a conclusion into a chain of reasoning, and explain why that protects both the child and the psychologist.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when someone points at a link and disagrees: consider it on its merits, and revise if they are right.",
"error": "Stating conclusions as fact without the inference that led to them.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Generate more than one hypothesis' and 'Test each hypothesis against the data'.",
"evidence":"Report — formulation · Log sheet tagged 2. Formulation"},

("Name and apply one framework to each substantial case","Include the child's construction of the problem"): {
"why":   "Every framework in the handbook has a place for the child's own account, and the Reference sheet notes it is the element most often left out of trainee reports. UNCRC Article 12 and CORU SoP 5.11 are the standards mapped here. The child's construction — what they think is hard, what helps, what they would change — should shape the formulation, not decorate it in a quote box. If it contradicts the adults' view, that contradiction is a finding.",
"s1":    "WATCH FOR — how your supervisor asks the child for their view: the questions, the tools (drawing, scaling, sorting), and where the answer ends up in the report.\n\nWRITE DOWN — the questions, and one example of the child's view changing the formulation.\n\nASK AFTERWARDS — 'when has a child's view changed your formulation?'",
"s2":    "YOU TAKE — gathering the child's view directly, using age-appropriate methods, and recording it in their words.\n\nTHEY HOLD — how the child's view is weighed in the formulation.\n\nWHY THAT SPLIT — gathering the view is direct work you can do with support. Weighing it against adult accounts is formulation judgement.",
"s3":    "YOU RUN IT — gather the child's view and write it into the formulation, showing what it changed.\n\nWHAT THEY ARE WATCHING FOR — the child's view in a box at the start, disconnected from the formulation.\n\nSTEPPING IN LOOKS LIKE — 'if you took the child's words out, would the formulation change? It should.'",
"s4":    "YOU DO IT ALONE — ask the child directly, record their construction in their words, and show in the formulation how it shaped your conclusions.\n\nPASS TEST — The child's view shapes it, not just decorates it\n\nEVIDENCE TO FILE — the formulation section. Log against 2. Formulation and 6. Ethical Practice.\n\nWHAT FAILURE LOOKS LIKE — a formulation built entirely from adult accounts, with a child quote added for colour.",
"s5":    "YOU CAN TEACH IT — you can explain Article 12, the methods for eliciting a child's view at different ages, and how to integrate it into each of the four frameworks.\n\nWHEN IT GOES WRONG — you can say what to do when the child's view contradicts everyone else's: treat it as data about how the difficulty is experienced, and let it shape the recommendations.",
"error": "Quoting the child for colour while building the formulation entirely from adult accounts.",
"feeds": "→ Feeds into 'Identify where sources diverge and say so', into Intervention planning, and into Ethical Practice — the child's voice.",
"evidence":"Report — formulation · Log sheet tagged 2. Formulation and 6. Ethical Practice"},

# BATCH9
}
